#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-pulse — the drive loop: every couple of hours the box wakes its own sessions, and each one decides for
itself whether there is work. Nothing else on the box starts development; every other timer reacts to something
that already happened. This is the one that makes the rest run continuously with no human poke.

  cc-pulse                       THE WHOLE MACHINE — every repo with a board; a live orch is enumerated and
                                 gets a verdict line, but is never pulsed (see DUE-NESS)
  cc-pulse <target> [<target>…]  exactly those
  cc-pulse --list                the targets this run would cover, one per line
  cc-pulse --dry-run             who would be woken and why, and everyone skipped and why; touches nothing
                                 (`--dry` is the same flag)
  cc-pulse selfcheck

TARGET is `<repo>` (a planning session) or `<repo>@<alias>` (an orch) — the tmux window name in session `main`,
which is also the address Slack routes on.

NO LIST TO MAINTAIN. The targets are enumerated live, every run, from ~/.cc/boards/*.json and the orch routing
table ~/.cc/slack/orchs.json. A repo registered or an orch spawned an hour ago is covered by the next pulse; a
hand-written list would have to be remembered by whoever adds the next one, and would not be.

A WAKE IS NOT FREE. Every tick is a full-context turn on a session carrying 200-400k of cache, and the machine
paid that for twelve sessions every 2 h — course and channel sessions among them — mostly to be told "nothing
due" (efficiency audit 2026-09-01). Measured again over 2026-09-02..08: $589 of pulse wakes, $91 a day, 175 of
them to planning seats at $2.54 each and 51 to orchs at $2.84, and the seat's own answer to most was "nothing
due, reply nothing". So a tick now goes only where the files say the seat can ACT — see DUE-NESS — and a target
with nothing to act on is looked at once a day instead of twelve times. The lifting of the head to the goals is
what that daily look-up is for: a session that runs only when a row is startable never does it, and that
lifting is the drive loop (owner, 2026-09-01). Past the gate the pulse still holds no opinion about the WORK: it
starts an idle session where there is none, types one line into every live one, and the SESSION judges. It
dispatches nothing, spends nothing, and writes no state but its own log (~/.cc/state/pulse.log), the quiet mark
below, and, once, a goals skeleton for a target that has none, so the tick never points at an empty file.

DUE-NESS IS READ OFF FILES — no session, no judgement — and it decides who is worth waking, not what the work
is (the one exception is a row carrying an `agent` mark, below, which costs one tmux listing):
  · a repo whose board rows are ALL kind=session is NEVER pulsed. A channel/course session has no track work,
    no brief and no done; the people in its channel are its loop.
  · AN ORCH IS NEVER PULSED EITHER (`<repo>@<alias>`). It is a project a person steers in its own channel, and
    that channel and its own workers are what wake it — the tick was 51 wakes in a week telling an orch with no
    board of its own what it had already told its channel.
  · a target IS woken when the files say there is something it can ACT ON now: main is red, a row STOPPED
    (`blocked` — what a landing that died leaves behind), a queued row could actually be STARTED (below), or an
    owner ask has sat past its line (CC_PULSE_ASK_H, 24 h — a fresher one arrived in a message the session read
    for itself). A tool that could not answer counts as actionable: a ledger that cannot be read is not a
    ledger saying "nothing open".
  · …and it is woken ONCE A DAY (CC_PULSE_IDLE_H) when none of that is true — rows in flight, asks inside their
    line, queued rows nothing can place. That look-up is the drive loop's, and the tick still CARRIES WHAT THE
    FILES SAY — the `main-gate` row, the open asks, the rows queued or blocked, and the rows still in flight (a
    PR in `review` above all) — so the session costs one cheap turn instead of re-deriving all of it. Every
    other tick on that path is one line in the log saying what it saw, and wakes nobody. The round is taken in
    pulse() below the liveness and limit tests, like the back-off and for the same reason: a seat that died
    inside it is still started, and one the limit stopped still reaches `cc-limit record`.
  · …and WHICH of those queued rows could actually be STARTED right now, from `cc-brief ready <repo>`: the ones
    nothing in flight is holding a file of, and the ones that share a file and so are one builder's work rather
    than two. A count of queued rows was never an answer to "is there room" — the width is capped by one writer
    per file, and working that out was left to whoever happened to look, so fifteen changes landed in seven hours
    while twenty rows sat queued and the box still looked idle. The tick carries the headline and the command for
    the rest; it is silent when nothing could start, and nothing here dispatches anything. That headline may say
    the answer is WITHHELD — a row in flight that nothing can place makes every "free" unsafe, and cc-brief says
    which row to give a branch to; withheld wakes the seat, because giving that row a branch is the work. It
    reaches GitHub for those rows only, which is the one call in this tick that leaves the box.
  · a row a LIVE session is doing in its own subagents is in flight, not work waiting to be picked up. It has
    no worker and no worktree anyone holds, so `queued` was offered for dispatch on every tick; the session
    marks it (`cc board set <repo> <track> agent me`) and the mark holds while that tmux window holds a
    session and the mark is still warm — CC_AGENT_MARK_HOURS, 6 h, refreshed by marking again. A mark made
    before the START of a usage limit that has since lifted is cold whatever its age: the subagent holding it
    died on the 429 (~/.cc/state/limit-resumed, written by `cc-limit resume`), so that row is offered again at
    once. A mark made after it is warm on its own age — the box works on the fallback for the rest of an episode.
  · THE SAME LOOK IS NOT SENT TWICE. Every tick writes what it carried to ~/.cc/state/<target>/pulse.look, and
    a tick whose look is identical to that one is not sent inside CC_PULSE_QUIET (default 24 h): it goes to the
    target's events file instead (`cc-broker ledger --once`), which wakes nobody and rides out on the next
    message the session does get. This holds an ACTIONABLE repeat and only that — main red yesterday and red
    still — so a look that changes INTO something to act on wakes at once, exactly as it always did. The other
    path is held by the daily floor above, not by this. The back-off is taken in pulse(), below the
    liveness and limit tests: a target whose session DIED is started whatever the mark says, a handoff clears
    it, and a session the limit stopped is still handed to `cc-limit record` before anything is held.
    Measured 2026-09-05: a planning session with one PR parked in `review` for two days paid a full-context
    wake every 2 h to answer "unchanged, no action" — 12 turns and $51 in one day, $126 across the box, for
    338 output tokens. This takes those twelve down to one, which is about $4.30, not nothing: the last of it
    is the idle CONTEXT itself, 200-400k re-written for one wake, and shedding that is cc-handoff's job.
Every one of those is one line in the log with its reason, and `cc-pulse --dry-run` prints the same verdicts
for every target while touching nothing — no tick, no log, no mark.
The pane it hands to `cc-limit` below is written down by cc-limit, in cc-limit's own file.

NO PILEUPS. A session gets at most one tick per cycle, and none at all while it is mid-handoff (a successor is
reading the journal — a tick would land on two sessions at once), while its pane is mid-turn (the last tick, or
whatever the owner asked, is still unanswered) or while a tick from a previous run is younger than the
gap below. Each of those is one line in the log and nothing else.

A SESSION THE USAGE LIMIT STOPPED is not ticked either — the tick would be answered by the same limit and the
turn it interrupted would still be lost. The pane is handed to `cc-limit record`, which writes it down and arms
a one-shot at the reset; cc-limit nudges it back to the turn it was on when the limit lifts. A pane cc-limit has
seen on the limit but is not recording yet (nothing corroborates it, or no reset time named) is left alone in
exactly the same way — it is a limited pane either way. See cc-limit.

A SESSION THAT NEVER STARTED IS NAMED on the way past. A launcher hands a session its purpose as its first
turn; when that turn is lost the window looks perfectly healthy and nobody is working in it — two orchs sat at
zero tokens on 2026-09-08 until a person typed at them. A target live for CC_PULSE_STALL_MIN minutes (20;
negative turns it off) with no finished turn on record is reported, and then ticked like any other: the tick is
the recovery, the finding is what was missing. It is a PLANNING SEAT's catch only, now that an orch takes no
tick — a never-started orch is its channel's to notice, and that is the price of not pulsing one at all. See
never_started().

THREE SWITCHES, because a loop that wakes the whole machine must be stoppable by whoever it wakes:
  CC_PULSE=0 in ~/.cc/config   the drive loop is off — no run does anything, dry or not
  ~/.cc/state/<target>/pulse.off   that one target is left alone; everything else still runs
  cc-pause on <repo>           that whole project is parked — its repo, its tracks and its orchs, not one target

THE GAP IS SHORTER THAN THE PERIOD ON PURPOSE (CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN in ~/.cc/config, default 90 min against a 2 h
timer). Equal, and a run that starts a second later than the one before it measures 1 h 59 m since its own last
tick and skips — every other cycle, forever, and the drive loop quietly halves itself.

Exit: 0 done, findings or not · 2 usage.
"""
import calendar
import fcntl
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
BIN = os.environ.get("CC_PULSE_BIN") or os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))   # test hook: stub siblings
BOARDS, STATE, DEV = f"{HOME}/.cc/boards", f"{HOME}/.cc/state", f"{HOME}/dev"
ORCHS = f"{HOME}/.cc/slack/orchs.json"          # {"<chat>": {target, alias, parent, …, archived}} — cc-slack owns it
LOG = f"{STATE}/pulse.log"
LOG_MAX = 4000                                  # a 2 h timer over a dozen targets fills this in about a month
GAP_MIN = 90                                    # minutes; see THE GAP above before changing it
STALL_MIN = 20                                  # minutes a live session may sit without EVER finishing a turn
                                                # before the pulse names it (CC_PULSE_STALL_MIN; negative = never).
                                                # Under the 20-minute mark a session is just thinking; past it,
                                                # nothing has happened since it was launched — see never_started()
QUIET_H = 24                                    # hours a session already told THIS EXACT LOOK is left alone
                                                # (CC_PULSE_QUIET; 0 turns the back-off off entirely). It was 6,
                                                # and it applied only to a look with nothing in it at all — so a
                                                # board that never moved bought a full-context turn every 2 h.
IDLE_H = 24                                     # hours between LOOK-UPS for a target with nothing to act on
                                                # (CC_PULSE_IDLE_H; 0 turns the floor off). The one wake left on
                                                # that path — #173's amendment, kept at once a day, not twelve.
ASK_H = 24                                      # hours an owner ask may sit unsettled before the pulse counts it
                                                # as past its line (CC_PULSE_ASK_H; negative = no ask ever is).
                                                # The session READ the message the ask was made in, so a fresh
                                                # one is already in its context and the pulse is not how it hears
                                                # of it. One it has left for a day is a different thing.
MAIN_GATE = "main-gate"                         # cc-audit's one row for "main's own suite is red"
SESSION_ROW = "session"                         # cc-board's kind for a long-lived channel session: never track work
DONE_ROW = ("merged", "done")                   # a row in one of these is history, not work
BOARD_DUE = ("queued", "blocked", "todo")       # work WAITING to be picked up; todo = older boards' spelling.
                                                # Everything else that is not DONE_ROW is work in flight — see due()
WORKING_FOOTER = "esc to interrupt"             # Claude Code's footer for the whole of a turn (same string cc-slack reads)
# A MODAL WAITING FOR A KEYPRESS IS CC-MSG'S PROBLEM, NOT THIS ONE'S. The pulse used to carry its own copy of
# the dialog strings and skip such a pane — which is exactly how, on 2026-09-01, twelve sessions sat behind a
# model-limit prompt and every tick aimed at them was thrown away silently. cc-msg is the one deliverer: it
# answers the prompts that are safe to answer, and queues the tick behind anything else until the pane is free.
NAME = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}(@[a-z0-9-]{2,20})?")

TICK = ("pulse: you are the independent engineer for {t}. Off the files, right now: {s}. Work the loop — "
        "WORKING.md order: red > undelivered asks > board todo/queued > goals (~/.cc/state/{t}/goals.md). "
        "Decide and act; the owner only for CLAUDE.md approval classes, via @-mention in Slack. Nothing due = "
        "do nothing, reply nothing.")

# The GOALS TICK: nothing on the board is due, and that is exactly the wake the owner's amendment keeps — a
# session that only runs when a row is queued never lifts its head. No "do nothing" clause here: told both
# "look at your goals" and "nothing due = do nothing" in one message, a session reads the second and the
# 6 h quiet mark makes the drive a no-op (review of #173).
TICK_GOALS = ("pulse: you are the independent engineer for {t}. Off the files, right now: {s}. Nothing on the "
              "board is due — this is the goals tick: read ~/.cc/state/{t}/goals.md and move one of them. "
              "Decide and act; the owner only for CLAUDE.md approval classes, via @-mention in Slack.")

GOALS = ("# goals — {t}\n\n"
         "no goals recorded yet — derive them from the channel's history and canvas, propose them to the\n"
         "owner via @-mention once, and keep this file as the answer.\n")


def now():
    return time.time()


def stamp(t=None):
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(t))


def epoch(s):
    """A log timestamp back to seconds; anything unreadable is 'long ago', never 'in the future'."""
    try:
        return float(calendar.timegm(time.strptime(str(s), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")))
    except ValueError:
        return 0.0


def limit_started():
    """When the last usage-limit episode cc-limit resumed from BEGAN (epoch), or 0. Written by `cc-limit resume`,
    "<started>\t<reset>\t<resumed>" — see WHAT ELSE THE LIMIT KILLED there. A mark older than that start was made
    before the 429, and the subagent holding it died on it. The START and not the reset: the box does not stop for
    the episode, cc-model moves every session to the fallback about a minute in, so a mark made after that is a
    subagent that is alive and working (review of #264). Field 2 is the reset, and an episode whose reset has not
    passed is ignored — only one that is over says anything about who survived it."""
    try:
        f = open(os.environ.get("CC_LIMIT_RESUMED") or f"{STATE}/limit-resumed").read().split("\n")[0].split("\t")
        if len(f) != 3:
            return 0.0          # not the three fields cc-limit writes: say nothing rather than read field 1 as a start
        at, until = float(f[0]), float(f[1])
        return at if 0 < at and 0 < until <= now() else 0.0
    except Exception:
        return 0.0


def conf(key, default):
    """A number from the environment, else ~/.cc/config, else the default. Same shape as every other knob."""
    val = os.environ.get(key, "")
    if not val:
        try:
            for ln in open(f"{HOME}/.cc/config"):
                if ln.strip().startswith(key + "="):
                    val = ln.split("=", 1)[1].strip().strip("\"'")
        except OSError:
            pass
    try:
        return int(val)
    except ValueError:
        return default


def sh(*cmd, timeout=120):
    """Run something and hand back the result, or None. A timer job never dies of a command that hung."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
    except Exception:
        return None


def why(p):
    out = ((p.stderr or "") + (p.stdout or "")).strip() if p is not None else ""
    if out:
        return out.splitlines()[-1]
    return "no answer" if p is None else f"exit {p.returncode}"


# ── what is actually there ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def windows():
    """name -> (window id, pane pid) in session main."""
    p = sh("tmux", "list-windows", "-t", "main", "-F", "#{window_id}\t#{window_name}\t#{pane_pid}", timeout=20)
    out = {}
    if p is not None and p.returncode == 0:
        for ln in p.stdout.splitlines():
            f = ln.split("\t")
            if len(f) >= 3 and f[1]:
                out[f[1]] = (f[0], f[2])
    return out


def agent_held(rows, repo):
    """The rows of `repo` a LIVE planning session has marked as its own subagent's work. Since 2026-09-04 that is
    how a backlog row is normally done: nothing on the box holds its worktree, so a row sitting in `queued` is
    offered for dispatch on every tick — offering work somebody is already doing. The session writes its own tmux
    window onto the row (`cc board set <repo> <track> agent me`, cc-board agent_id) and the mark holds on the same
    two tests cc-reconcile makes: an interactive `claude` is in that window (live_claude — a pane left with a
    `tail -f` in it is not a session), and the window is named for the row's own repo, since a session speaks for
    its repo and no other and this board is a file every session can write.
    A COLD MARK HOLDS NOTHING EITHER, on the same bounds cc-reconcile uses: a session that marked a row and then
    moved on without clearing it would otherwise keep the row out of every tick for as long as its window lived,
    and nothing here can see whether its subagent is still running. Marking again refreshes the time. A mark made
    before the START of a usage limit that has since lifted is cold at any age — see below.
    LAZY ON PURPOSE: a board with no marked row costs neither a tmux nor a ps. A tmux that cannot answer holds
    NOTHING — the row goes back to being offered, which is what this file did before the mark existed."""
    # …AND A USAGE LIMIT MAKES A MARK COLD BEFORE ITS TIME. On 2026-09-05 a limit at 09:05Z killed a session's
    # subagents mid-row; their marks stayed warm until 15:05Z, so rows nobody was doing were held out of every
    # tick for nearly four hours after the limit lifted at 11:10Z. limit_started() is when that episode BEGAN —
    # the 429 that killed them — and not when it reset: the box goes on working on the fallback in between, so a
    # mark made after the start is a live subagent's (review of #264). It can only ever move the bound later, and
    # a mark made after the box came back is warm on its own age, as it always was. A mark made AT the start
    # second is cold, the same way one exactly CC_AGENT_MARK_HOURS old is (`>`, not `>=`): that second is the one
    # the limit was first seen in. cc-reconcile's mark_why() decides that second the same way.
    cold = max(now() - float(os.environ.get("CC_AGENT_MARK_HOURS") or 6) * 3600, limit_started())
    marks = {k: (r.get("agent") or "").strip() for k, r in rows.items()
             if (r.get("agent") or "").strip() and epoch(r.get("agent_at")) > cold}
    if not marks:
        return set()
    p = sh("tmux", "list-windows", "-t", "main", "-F", "#{window_id}:#{pid}\t#{window_name}\t#{pane_pid}", timeout=20)
    wins = {}
    if p is not None and p.returncode == 0:
        for ln in p.stdout.splitlines():
            f = ln.split("\t")
            # `<repo>` is a session a person steers; `<repo>@<alias>` is an orchestrator in the same checkout,
            # on the same board and just as entitled to hold a row (cc-reconcile mark_why reads it the same way).
            if len(f) >= 3 and f[0].strip() and (f[1] == repo or f[1].startswith(repo + "@")):
                wins[f[0].strip()] = f[2].strip()
    alive = {w for w in set(marks.values()) if w in wins and live_claude(wins[w])}
    return {k for k, w in marks.items() if w in alive}


def live_claude(pane_pid):
    """Is there an interactive `claude` ANYWHERE under the pane's shell? cc-msg asks the narrower question —
    a DIRECT child — and that is wrong here in the one case that matters: a session started by
    `cc-handoff --overlap` runs `bash -c '… cc __runnext …'`, so its claude is a grandchild, and the direct-child
    test calls the pane dead. Dead means rule 1, and rule 1 would start a SECOND session on a repo that already
    has one — the very collision rule 4 exists to prevent, and it has been seen on a real box.
    `-p` is excluded because a headless worker's claude is not something you can type at; a bare shell has no
    claude under it at all."""
    p = sh("ps", "-eo", "pid=,ppid=,comm=", timeout=20)
    if p is None or p.returncode != 0:
        return False
    kids, name = {}, {}
    for ln in p.stdout.splitlines():
        f = ln.split(None, 2)
        if len(f) == 3:
            kids.setdefault(f[1], []).append(f[0])
            name[f[0]] = f[2].strip()
    seen, stack = set(), list(kids.get(str(pane_pid), []))
    while stack:
        pid = stack.pop()
        if pid in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(pid)
        if name.get(pid) == "claude" and not headless(pid):
            return True
        stack.extend(kids.get(pid, []))
    return False


def headless(pid):
    """A `claude -p` prints and exits; there is no prompt to type a tick into."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb") as f:
            argv = f.read().split(b"\0")
    except OSError:
        return False
    return b"-p" in argv or b"--print" in argv


def pane_age(pane_pid):
    """Seconds since that pane's process started, which is near enough the launch: `cc` opens the window ON the
    command that becomes this pid, so the pane is exactly as old as the session under it. Read with `ps`, and no
    launch stamp of our own — a file written at every launch is one more thing to keep, prune and get wrong, and
    a session restarted in place would carry a stale one. None when ps cannot say."""
    p = sh("ps", "-o", "etimes=", "-p", str(pane_pid), timeout=20)
    if p is None or p.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        return int(p.stdout.strip())
    except ValueError:
        return None


def never_started(target, pane_pid, r):
    """Has this session been sitting there since it was launched without ever finishing a turn? Minutes if so,
    else None — and None too whenever anything below cannot answer, because a tool that is unwell must never
    become a finding about a session that is working fine.

    WHY THIS IS ASKED AT ALL. A session is launched WITH a purpose — `cc <repo> --orch <alias> "<brief>"` hands
    it as the session's first turn — and a launch that loses that turn leaves a window that looks perfectly
    healthy with nobody working in it. On 2026-09-08 two orchs came up with their system prompt and nothing
    else and sat at zero tokens until a person typed at them; the owner noticed, the box did not. Nothing else
    asks it: cc-started watches the first 90 s and only ever asks whether a PROMPT WAS DRAWN, which it was.

    TWO FACTS, NO NEW STATE. How old the pane is, and when this session last FINISHED a turn — cc-context's
    record store is written by the Stop hook and by nothing else, so a record is proof of a completed turn and
    its age says when. A record older than the pane belongs to whatever ran in that window before this session.
    Column one of `cc-context --all` is the session's address (`<repo>` and `<repo>@<alias>` are two sessions in
    one checkout), column two its track, column seven the record's age in seconds."""
    stall = conf("CC_PULSE_STALL_MIN", STALL_MIN)
    if stall < 0:
        return None
    age = pane_age(pane_pid)
    if age is None or age < stall * 60:
        return None
    seen = r.turns()
    if seen is None:                                # cc-context could not answer: say nothing rather than guess
        return None
    last = seen.get(target)
    if last is not None and last <= age:            # a turn finished since this pane started: it got going
        return None
    return age // 60


def screen(wid):
    """What that pane shows right now. One capture answers both questions asked of it — no LLM, just strings."""
    p = sh("tmux", "capture-pane", "-p", "-t", wid, timeout=20)
    return p.stdout if (p is not None and p.returncode == 0) else ""


def working(s):
    """Is that pane mid-turn? The same test cc-slack makes."""
    return WORKING_FOOTER in s


def limit_state(wid, target, dry):
    """Is this session sitting on the usage limit? cc-limit owns the whole test — the line, the corroboration that
    it is a real limit and not prose, and the reset time — and `record` writes <pane, window, reset> down and arms
    the one-shot that nudges the session when the limit lifts.
    Its exit code is the answer, and 1 is the ONLY one that means "type here": 0 = recorded, 2 = the limit line is
    on that pane but cc-limit is not recording it yet (nothing corroborates it, or no reset time has been named).
    Reading 2 as a plain failure is how a limited session gets ticked, which is what this whole branch prevents.
    A cc-limit that cannot answer at all reads as 1, deliberately: the drive loop must not stop waking the box
    because one CLI is unwell, and the worst a tick costs is one line read by a session that is busy."""
    p = sh(f"{BIN}/cc-limit", "record", *(["--dry"] if dry else []), wid, target, timeout=30)
    return p.returncode if p is not None else 1


def handoff_open(target):
    """True open · False none · None cc-handoff could not answer. It owns the whole handoff lifecycle; this
    only asks, and a target it has never heard of (an orch) is simply a target with no record."""
    p = sh(f"{BIN}/cc-handoff", "--status", target, timeout=60)
    if p is None:
        return None
    return True if p.returncode == 0 else (False if p.returncode == 1 else None)


def discover():
    """Every target on the box, right now: a board file is a repo, a live row in the orch table is an orch."""
    out = []
    for f in sorted(glob.glob(f"{BOARDS}/*.json")):
        repo = os.path.basename(f)[:-5]
        if not repo.startswith("_") and NAME.fullmatch(repo):
            out.append(repo)
    try:
        table = json.load(open(ORCHS))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        table = {}
    for e in (table.values() if isinstance(table, dict) else []):
        if not isinstance(e, dict) or e.get("archived"):        # archived = that orch is retired, not asleep
            continue
        t = f"{e.get('target', '')}@{e.get('alias', '')}"
        if NAME.fullmatch(t) and t not in out:
            out.append(t)
    return out


def last_tick(target):
    """When this target was last woken, read off the log — which is the only record the pulse keeps."""
    try:
        lines = open(LOG).read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return 0.0
    for ln in reversed(lines):
        f = ln.split(None, 3)
        if len(f) >= 3 and f[1] == target and f[2] == "tick":
            return epoch(f[0])
    return 0.0


# ── is anything due? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def board_read(path):
    """A board file, read through cc-board's opener (imported, the way cc-time is): a member's board is a link into a
    directory the MEMBER writes, and a plain open here followed whatever it planted — a pipe hung this tool for good
    (review of #224). None = no board there; cc-board's Refused for anything planted, which a caller treats as unreadable."""
    if not hasattr(board_read, "mod"):
        import importlib.machinery, importlib.util
        s = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("ccboard", importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
            "ccboard", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-board")))   # beside THIS copy, not a stubbed BIN
        board_read.mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(s); s.loader.exec_module(board_read.mod)
    return board_read.mod.read(path)


def board_rows(repo):
    """The repo's board rows, {name: row}. No board — or one half-written under us — is no rows, and no rows is
    not a channel repo: a repo whose board is empty is still driven, it just has nothing in the tick."""
    try:
        rows = (board_read(f"{BOARDS}/{repo}.json") or {}).get("tracks")
    except Exception:
        return {}
    return {k: v for k, v in rows.items() if isinstance(v, dict)} if isinstance(rows, dict) else {}


def kind(row):
    """A row written before kinds existed carries no field and IS a track — cc-board reads it the same way."""
    return row.get("kind") or "track"


def open_asks(repo):
    """(unsettled owner asks, how many of those are PAST THEIR LINE) from cc-scope, which owns that ledger.
    (-1, 0) = it could not say.

    ONE call where there were two: `cc-scope count` is the length of this same list, and the ages are the half a
    count cannot give. The line is CC_PULSE_ASK_H — see ASK_H: an ask inside it is one the session read for
    itself, and waking it to say so is the wake this file is trying to stop paying for."""
    p = sh(f"{BIN}/cc-scope", "list", repo, "--json", timeout=60)
    if p is None or p.returncode != 0:
        return -1, 0
    try:
        asks = json.loads(p.stdout or "")
    except ValueError:
        return -1, 0
    if not isinstance(asks, list):
        return -1, 0
    line = conf("CC_PULSE_ASK_H", ASK_H) * 3600
    if line < 0:
        return len(asks), 0
    # epoch() reads an unparseable stamp as "long ago", which here means past the line: a row whose date nobody
    # can read is one nobody can say is fresh, and the cheap answer to that is a wake, not a silence.
    old = sum(1 for a in asks if isinstance(a, dict) and now() - epoch(a.get("asked")) > line)
    return len(asks), old


def last_look(target):
    """(what the last tick carried, when it carried it) — the mark the pulse leaves behind so it can tell a look
    it has already sent from a new one. ("", 0) = there has been no tick, and nothing is held against it."""
    f = f"{STATE}/{target}/pulse.look"
    try:
        return open(f).read().strip(), os.path.getmtime(f)
    except OSError:
        return "", 0.0


def set_look(target, look):
    """Write down what this tick carried, and when. Never on a dry run — see Run.dry."""
    f = f"{STATE}/{target}/pulse.look"
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(f), exist_ok=True)
        with open(f, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(look + "\n")
    except OSError:
        pass                                        # a mark nobody can write costs one extra wake, nothing more


def clear_look(target):
    """Forget what the last tick carried. A session that is being STARTED, or one being handed over, has heard
    none of it — the mark is a record of what somebody was told, and there is no longer anybody it is true of."""
    try:
        os.remove(f"{STATE}/{target}/pulse.look")
    except OSError:
        pass


def ledger(target, note):
    """Say it where it wakes nobody. cc-broker owns the events file and the promise that its unread lines ride
    out on the next message the session does get, so the suppressed tick is recorded and never dropped."""
    sh(f"{BIN}/cc-broker", "ledger", "--once", target, f"pulse: {note}", timeout=30)


def due(target):
    """(wake?, why, what the files say, was-anything-due, the look, held?, floored?) — the whole decision, off
    files, before any session is looked at. Two questions, in this order: is there anything here the seat can
    ACT ON right now (see `actionable`), and if so would the tick say anything the session does not already
    have (see THE SAME LOOK). A target with nothing to act on is not dropped — it gets the look-up that keeps
    the drive loop, once a day rather than every couple of hours.

    `held` is the same-look back-off and ONLY that: a back-off from repeating ourselves to a session that heard
    us the first time. It assumes there is such a session, which is why pulse() takes it below the liveness test
    and not here — a target whose session died must still be started, and a 24 h mark must not be what stops
    that.

    `floored` is the once-a-day round, and it is a SEPARATE flag for the same reason and one more. Same reason:
    it too assumes a session that was looked at, so a seat that has since died must still be started and a
    limited one must still reach `cc-limit record` — both of which pulse() only does below. One more: unlike a
    held tick it repeats nothing the session was told, so nothing goes to its events file. That is the whole
    difference between the two flags at the bottom of pulse().

    Every other False is a target this loop has no business waking at all, live or dead."""
    repo = target.split("@")[0]                     # pulse() turns an orch away before this, so the split is
                                                    # a no-op here — kept so due() answers for any target shape
    rows = board_rows(repo)

    # A CHANNEL/COURSE REPO IS NEVER PULSED. Its rows are `session` — a long-lived session in a channel, with no
    # brief, no PR and no done. There is no track work to drive, and the people in the channel are its loop.
    if rows and all(kind(r) == SESSION_ROW for r in rows.values()):
        return (False, "every board row is kind=session — a channel repo's sessions are driven by their channel",
                "", False, "", False, False)

    gate = rows.get(MAIN_GATE)
    red = gate is not None and gate.get("status") not in DONE_ROW
    asks, old = open_asks(repo)

    # A ROW THAT IS NOT HISTORY IS SOMETHING TO LOOK AT, and the two kinds are different sentences. Queued or
    # blocked is work WAITING to be picked up; `review`, `running` and `waiting` are work ALREADY IN FLIGHT —
    # and the commonest of those is a PR sitting in `review`, which is what cc-reconcile writes for as long as
    # one is open. Counting only the first set told that session "goals are the work" and then muted it for
    # CC_PULSE_QUIET, so an unlanded PR lost its nudge for 6 h and the session was steered into new goal work
    # instead of landing it. Anything that is neither history nor queued counts as in flight, so a status word
    # cc-board grows later rounds UP to a wake rather than silently reading as nothing due.
    # …AND A ROW A LIVE SESSION IS DOING IN ITS OWN SUBAGENTS IS IN FLIGHT, whatever word it carries. Nothing on
    # the box holds its worktree, so `queued` reads as work waiting to be picked up and every tick offered it for
    # a second dispatch. agent_held() is the whole test, and it fails back to today's answer: a mark whose window
    # is gone counts for nothing and the row is offered again.
    # …AND NEITHER IS A ROW A PERSON IS DELIBERATELY SITTING ON (cc-board's `held`): same failure, a different
    # cause. `queued` is the only word an undispatched row can wear, so a row somebody stopped on purpose read
    # as work waiting to be picked up and was offered for dispatch on every tick. It stays IN FLIGHT rather than
    # disappearing — a hold is not history, and the only thing that releases it is a person lifting it.
    held = agent_held({k: r for k, r in rows.items() if kind(r) != SESSION_ROW}, repo)
    on_hold = {k for k, r in rows.items() if kind(r) != SESSION_ROW and str(r.get("held") or "").strip()}
    taken = held | on_hold
    board = [(k, r.get("status") or "queued")       # cc-board's own default for a row written without one
             for k, r in rows.items() if kind(r) != SESSION_ROW]
    ready = sorted(k for k, st in board if st in BOARD_DUE and k not in taken)
    # …and the same rows WITH the word each is wearing, for the look below. The note prints them bare to stay
    # one line, and bare is not enough to compare on: `queued` → `blocked` is a row that stopped, and reading
    # that as unchanged would hold the wake that says so.
    ready_st = sorted(f"{k} {st}" for k, st in board if st in BOARD_DUE and k not in taken)
    # …and the ones that STOPPED. The brief: "a decision-class event (red main, a stopped landing, an ask past
    # its line) must still reach the seat within one tick as today". `blocked` is the word a stop wears —
    # cc-reconcile writes it for a row with no worker and nothing saying why — and a stop is a decision for the
    # seat whatever cc-brief thinks of the row's files, so it is read here and not through the room arithmetic.
    stopped = sorted(k for k, st in board if st == "blocked" and k not in taken)
    # …AND WHICH OF THOSE COULD ACTUALLY BE STARTED RIGHT NOW. A count of queued rows is not an answer to "is
    # there room": the width is capped by one writer per file, and working out which rows collide with nothing
    # in flight was left to whoever happened to look. Fifteen changes landed in seven hours while twenty rows
    # sat queued and the box looked idle (owner, 2026-09-07). cc-brief owns that arithmetic — it is the board's
    # rows against git's answer about each live branch — and this hands it the rows it has already decided are
    # unheld, because only this file can test an `agent` mark's window. It reports and starts nothing; the
    # session reads the headline and runs `cc-brief ready <repo>` for the rest.
    # IT IS NOT IN THE LOOK, on purpose. The look decides whether a wake happens at all, and this figure moves
    # every time a live branch commits a file some queued row happens to name — a wake per commit, at a full
    # context each, to say the room changed by one. The event that really opens the room is a row leaving flight,
    # and the look already carries that. So this rides the ticks that were going out anyway.
    #   …AND THE COARSE ANSWER — free, withheld, none, unread — IS in it, which is not a contradiction of the
    # paragraph above. That flag moves when the ROOM opens or closes and at no other time, so it costs a wake
    # when a row becomes startable and nothing when a live branch commits one more file.
    could, start = "", "none"
    if ready:
        out = sh(f"{BIN}/cc-brief", "ready", repo, *ready, timeout=120)
        # A TOOL THAT COULD NOT ANSWER IS NOT A TOOL SAYING "NO ROOM", and a crash is that as much as a timeout
        # is — the non-zero exit was read as an empty answer, so a broken cc-brief read as "no room" and every
        # queued row waited on the daily floor with the seat told nothing at all.
        if out is None or out.returncode != 0:
            could = ("cc-brief ready " + ("timed out" if out is None else f"failed: {why(out)}")
                     + " — which rows could start is unread this tick")
            start = "unread"
        else:
            head = ((out.stdout or "").strip().splitlines() or [""])[0]
            could = head.split(": ", 1)[1] if ": " in head else ""
            # cc-brief's two answers, told apart by its own words: rows it can call free, or an answer it
            # WITHHELD because a row in flight is unplaced. Withheld is work for the seat — cc-brief names the
            # row to give a branch to — so it wakes; no answer at all is no room, and wakes nobody.
            start = "free" if "could start now" in could else "withheld" if could else "none"
    aloft = sorted(f"{k} {st}" + (" (on hold)" if k in on_hold else " (a subagent has it)" if k in held else "")
                   for k, st in board if k not in ready and st not in DONE_ROW)
    note = " · ".join(filter(None, [
        "main-gate row RED" if red else "",
        (f"{asks} owner ask{'' if asks == 1 else 's'} open" if asks > 0 else
         "cc-scope could not count the open asks" if asks < 0 else ""),
        f"{len(ready)} row{'' if len(ready) == 1 else 's'} queued/blocked ({', '.join(ready[:3])})" if ready else "",
        f"{could} (cc-brief ready {repo})" if could else "",
        f"{len(aloft)} row{'' if len(aloft) == 1 else 's'} in flight ({', '.join(aloft[:3])})" if aloft else "",
    ])) or "no red main, no open ask, no row queued or in flight — goals are the work"
    # …and this one picks the tick's TEXT, not whether there is a tick: `anything` false is the GOALS tick and
    # nothing else. What decides a wake is `actionable`, below. (A ledger that could not answer counts as
    # something too, so the tick says so rather than reading as "no asks".)
    anything = bool(red or asks != 0 or ready or aloft)

    # THE SAME LOOK IS NOT NEWS, and a wake that carries nothing new costs exactly what one that does costs: a
    # full-context turn, 200-400k of cache re-written because the last tick's copy expired an hour before this
    # one. Measured 2026-09-05: about $4.30 a tick, twelve of them in a day nobody worked, and one that cost
    # $12.92 on its own because its second turn missed the cache too — all of it to answer "Unchanged. No
    # action." about a PR that had been sitting in `review` for two days.
    #   The look is the FULL state; `note` is the sentence the session reads. They differ on purpose — the note
    # truncates to three row names and drops their statuses so the tick stays one line, so two boards differing
    # only in the fourth row, or in one row's word, share a note. Deduping on the note would hide a real change;
    # deduping on this cannot.
    #   #173's finding is not undone by any of it. A row in flight still never sends the session to its goals
    # tick — it carries the work note instead, on whichever wake it gets. Held is not dropped either: pulse()
    # writes it to the target's events file, where cc-broker carries it out on the next message the session
    # gets for a real reason. The mark only ever holds an ACTIONABLE repeat; what holds the other path is the
    # once-a-day floor below, which is a different question and reads a different file.
    look = (f"red={int(red)} asks={asks}/{old} start={start} "
            f"ready={','.join(ready_st)} aloft={','.join(aloft)}")

    # WHAT THE SEAT CAN ACT ON NOW is a narrower question than "is anything on the files", and it is the one
    # that decides a wake. Measured over 2026-09-02..08: 175 planning wakes at $2.54 each, $91 a day, and the
    # seat's own answer to most of them was "nothing due, reply nothing" (owner, 2026-09-08: "make curbing
    # spend a number one priority"). Three things a seat can do something about the moment it reads them:
    #   · main is red — the gate row is the whole box's stop
    #   · a row STOPPED — `blocked`, which is what a landing that died leaves behind
    #   · a queued row could actually be STARTED, or cc-brief withheld the answer because a row in flight needs
    #     a branch, or it could not answer at all (a tool that cannot say is not a tool saying "no room")
    #   · an owner ask has sat past CC_PULSE_ASK_H, or the ledger could not be read
    # What is NOT here is deliberate. A row in flight has a worker or an open PR and lands itself; an ask made
    # an hour ago arrived in a message this session read; a queued row nothing can place is not room. Each of
    # those was a wake, and each one bought the answer "unchanged, no action".
    actionable = bool(red or stopped or start in ("free", "withheld", "unread") or old or asks < 0)

    seen, when = last_look(target)
    window = conf("CC_PULSE_QUIET", QUIET_H) * 3600
    same = bool(window and seen == look and when and now() - when < window)
    ago = f"{int((now() - when) // 60)}m ago" if when else "already"

    if actionable:
        if same:
            return (False, f"the tick {ago} carried this same look and nothing has changed since — it goes to "
                           f"the target's events file, which wakes nobody", note, True, look, True, False)
        return True, note, note, anything, look, False, False

    # NOTHING TO ACT ON IS STILL A LOOK-UP — once a day. The brief: "a repo with no live rows and no open asks
    # is looked at once a day, not twelve times". That is #173's amendment and this path's only wake: a
    # session that runs solely when a row is startable never lifts its head to its goals, and that lifting is
    # the drive loop. The floor is measured against the last TICK and not against the look, because on this
    # path the look moves for reasons none of which are worth a turn — a row's word changing from `running` to
    # `review` is a thing the seat can read later, out of the ledger, on a wake it was getting anyway.
    floor = conf("CC_PULSE_IDLE_H", IDLE_H) * 3600
    last = last_tick(target)
    if floor and last and now() - last < floor:
        # …and this skip is decided BELOW, like the back-off and for the same reasons: see `floored` above.
        return (False, f"nothing to act on — {note} — and the look-up {int((now() - last) // 60)}m ago was "
                       f"inside the {int(floor // 3600)}h round", note, anything, look, False, True)
    if anything:
        return (True, f"the once-a-day look-up, nothing on the files to act on: {note}", note, True, look,
                False, False)
    return True, "nothing due — the drive tick: the session looks up at its goals", note, False, look, False, False


# ── the run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
class Run:
    """One pass. Holds the dry-run flag and the tally so every target reports the same way."""

    def __init__(self, dry=False):
        self.dry, self.n, self._turns = dry, {}, None

    def turns(self):
        """{address: seconds since its last finished turn} for every session cc-context has a record of, read
        ONCE a run — a dozen targets must not mean a dozen forks. None when cc-context could not answer."""
        if self._turns is None:
            p = sh(f"{BIN}/cc-context", "--all", timeout=30)
            if p is None or p.returncode != 0:
                self._turns = False
            else:
                idx = {}
                for ln in p.stdout.splitlines():
                    f = ln.split("\t")
                    if len(f) < 7 or f[1] != "-":   # column two is a TRACK's name; a session a person steers,
                        continue                    # and an orch, both carry "-" there
                    try:
                        a = int(f[6])
                    except ValueError:
                        continue
                    if f[0] not in idx or a < idx[f[0]]:
                        idx[f[0]] = a
                self._turns = idx
        return None if self._turns is False else self._turns

    def log(self, target, verb, msg):
        self.n[verb] = self.n.get(verb, 0) + 1
        print(f"  {target:<24} {verb:<6} {msg}")
        if self.dry:
            return
        try:
            os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
            with open(LOG, "a") as f:
                f.write(f"{stamp()} {target} {verb} — {msg}\n")
        except OSError:
            pass                                    # a log nobody can write must not stop the loop it drives

    def trim(self):
        if self.dry:
            return
        try:
            lines = open(LOG).read().splitlines(True)
            if len(lines) > LOG_MAX:
                with open(LOG + ".tmp", "w") as f:
                    f.writelines(lines[-LOG_MAX:])
                os.replace(LOG + ".tmp", LOG)
        except OSError:
            pass


def ensure_goals(target, r):
    """Rule 3: a tick points at ~/.cc/state/<target>/goals.md, so a target with no such file gets a skeleton
    that says how to fill it. The one file outside its own log that the pulse ever writes, and only when
    there is none — an existing goals file is the session's, and is never touched."""
    p = f"{STATE}/{target}/goals.md"
    if os.path.exists(p):
        return
    if r.dry:
        r.log(target, "goals", f"no goals file — would write the skeleton at {p}")
        return
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
        with open(p, "x") as f:
            f.write(GOALS.format(t=target))
    except OSError:
        return
    r.log(target, "goals", "no goals file — wrote the skeleton, so the tick points at something")


def paused(target):
    """`cc-pause on <repo>` parks a whole project — its repo, its tracks and its orchs — and nothing the box does
    on its own may spend on it until `cc-pause off`. cc-pause owns that state; asking it is the only reading of it
    there is. It cannot run (no such sibling on this box, a timeout): behave exactly as this file did before the
    pause existed — a pause must never be the reason a pass stops working."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-pause", "is", str(target)], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                              capture_output=True, timeout=10).returncode == 0
    except Exception:
        return False


def opted_out(target):
    """`touch ~/.cc/state/<target>/pulse.off` and the pulse leaves that one alone. A session that wants to be
    left alone, or an owner who wants one repo quiet, must not have to switch the whole drive loop off."""
    return os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/{target}/pulse.off")


def pulse(target, r):
    """One target: start it if it is not there, tick it if it is, skip it if a tick would pile up."""
    repo, orch = target.split("@")[0], "@" in target
    if opted_out(target):
        r.log(target, "skip", "pulse.off — this target has opted out of the drive loop")
        return
    # A PROJECT PAUSE IS WIDER THAN AN OPT-OUT: pulse.off is keyed on the target, so `beta` and `beta@scout` are
    # separate files, while `cc-pause on beta` parks both — cc-pause reads the repo out of either shape. Before
    # ensure_goals, which is the one file the pulse writes into a parked project's state dir.
    if paused(target):
        r.log(target, "skip", f"paused — `cc-pause off {repo}` puts the whole project back")
        return
    # AN ORCH IS NOT PULSED AT ALL. It is a project a person steers in its own channel, and it is woken by the
    # two things that actually carry its work: a message in that channel, and its own workers reporting back.
    # A drive tick on top of those was 51 wakes over 2026-09-02..08 at $2.84 each, and the answer to a tick an
    # orch has no board of its own to read is the answer it already gave its channel. Above ensure_goals on
    # purpose: a goals file for a target nothing points at is a file nobody reads.
    #   Below `paused` on purpose too — `cc-pause on <repo>` parks the repo AND its orchs, and a pause is the
    # louder fact about a target, so the log says the pause and not this.
    #   WHAT THIS COSTS, named rather than glossed: the rounds below are also the only thing that hands an
    # ORCH's limited pane to `cc-limit record`, and the only thing that names an orch that never took its first
    # turn. Both go with the tick. An orch's channel is the loop that has to notice either now.
    if orch:
        r.log(target, "skip", "an orch is not pulsed — its channel and its own workers wake it, and restarting "
                              "one is not this loop's either (a fresh one mints a new Slack channel)")
        return
    ensure_goals(target, r)

    # THE CHEAPEST GATE RUNS FIRST. Everything below this line costs a tmux capture, a walk of the process table
    # or, at the end, a whole turn of somebody's context; this costs a few stat()s and one `cc-scope count`.
    wake, why_due, note, anything, look, held, floored = due(target)
    if not wake and not (held or floored):
        r.log(target, "skip", why_due)
        return
    # A HELD TICK IS NOT DECIDED YET, AND NEITHER IS A FLOORED ONE. The back-off says "this session already heard
    # this"; the round says "this session was looked at recently". Both assume a session, so both are taken
    # below, once a live one is actually there and the limit test has had its say. Taken here, either swallows
    # the START branch — a target whose planning session died, with a PR parked in `review`, would go unstarted
    # for the whole window — and the limit branch with it, which is worse: see the note down there. The floor
    # said all that and was still taken here (review of #390): for up to CC_PULSE_IDLE_H a dead seat went
    # unstarted and a limit-stopped one never reached `cc-limit record`, which is the #246 loss again.

    # THE HANDOFF CHECK COMES AHEAD OF THE WINDOW being looked at at all. It used to run only once the
    # window was found live, and that order is a trap: a target mid-overlap whose predecessor claude has already
    # exited (the window stays — `cc __runmain` ends in `exec bash`), or one caught in the kill→rename gap of a
    # retire, reads as DEAD and takes the start branch. `cc <repo>` then puts a fresh un-gated session beside the
    # gated successor, the handoff sweep reads the newcomer as "the predecessor came back", and the successor
    # halfway through the journal is expired and killed.
    h = handoff_open(target)
    if h:
        # …and the mark goes with it. The successor reading the journal is a session that has heard none of our
        # ticks, and a back-off left standing would mute it for up to a day over what its predecessor was told.
        if not r.dry:
            clear_look(target)
        r.log(target, "skip", "mid-handoff — a successor is taking over; neither a tick nor a start belongs here")
        return

    w = windows().get(target)
    if w is None or not live_claude(w[1]):
        if not r.dry:
            clear_look(target)              # nobody is there to have heard the last tick; whatever starts is new
        if h is None:
            # Asymmetric on purpose. A tick sent while cc-handoff is unwell costs one line read by a session
            # that may be busy; a START costs a whole second session on a repo that may be mid-handoff. Only
            # the cheap half of this job is done blind.
            r.log(target, "report", "no live session, and cc-handoff could not say whether one is being handed "
                                    "over — not starting a second one blind")
            return
        if not os.path.isdir(f"{DEV}/{repo}"):
            r.log(target, "skip", f"a board with no ~/dev/{repo} — nothing to start")
            return
        if r.dry:
            r.log(target, "start", f"no live session — would start an idle one: {why_due}" if w is None else
                  "the window holds no session — would ask `cc` for one, which it refuses while that pane "
                  "still has a child")
            return
        p = sh(f"{BIN}/cc", repo, timeout=120)
        if p is None or p.returncode != 0:
            r.log(target, "report", f"could not start a session: {why(p)}")
            return
        w2 = windows().get(target)
        if w2 is not None and live_claude(w2[1]):
            r.log(target, "start", f"no live session — started an idle one ({why_due}); the next pulse ticks it")
        else:
            # `cc <repo>` replaces a window only when its pane has NO child at all, and the shell that
            # `cc __runmain` execs when claude exits IS a child. So a window left behind by a dead session is
            # not replaced by anything, and "started an idle one" would be a lie repeated every 2 h forever.
            # Nobody sweeps a leftover PLANNING window either (cc-janitor only kills track windows), so the one
            # line the pulse writes says what has to happen for it to start anything.
            r.log(target, "report", "the window holds no session, and `cc` will not replace a window whose pane "
                                    "still has a child — close it and the next pulse starts one")
        return

    if h is None:
        # Ticking anyway is the deliberate choice: a pulse that cannot fire while one CLI is unwell stops the
        # whole box's development, and the worst a tick costs is one line read by a session that is busy.
        r.log(target, "report", "cc-handoff could not answer — ticking anyway")
    scr = screen(w[0])
    if working(scr):
        r.log(target, "skip", "mid-turn — the last tick is still unanswered")
        return
    # After the mid-turn test on purpose: a session still working is not one the limit stopped, whatever its
    # scrollback says.
    lim = limit_state(w[0], target, r.dry)
    if lim == 0:
        r.log(target, "skip", "the usage limit stopped it mid-turn — recorded; cc-limit nudges it back to that "
                              "turn at the reset, and a tick now would only meet the same limit")
        return
    if lim == 2:
        r.log(target, "skip", "the usage limit line is on its pane and cc-limit is not recording it yet (nothing "
                              "corroborates it, or no reset time named) — a tick would meet that same limit")
        return

    # NAMED, NEVER ACTED ON. A session that never took its first turn is reported here and then ticked by the
    # normal path below, which is the recovery this file already has; the finding is what was missing. Below the
    # limit test on purpose — a session the limit stopped on its opening turn is that, not a lost launch.
    stalled = never_started(target, w[1], r)
    if stalled is not None:
        r.log(target, "report", f"live for {stalled}m and has never finished a turn — whatever launched it left "
                                f"it with no first turn to work on (tmux select-window -t main:{target})")

    # THE BACK-OFF, now that there is a live session it can be true of, and BELOW the limit test on purpose.
    # `limit_state()` above is the only thing on the box that hands an interactive session to `cc-limit record`,
    # which is what arms the one-shot that nudges it back to its interrupted turn at the reset. Held above that
    # call, a session the limit stopped mid-turn changes nothing on the board, so its look never changes, so
    # every pulse for the whole quiet window returned here and cc-limit was never told — the turn was lost for
    # a day and then for good, once cc-limit's corroboration had expired (review of #246).
    # A HELD TICK IS STILL RECORDED — but only one with something in it, since the goals tick's "nothing at
    # all" is nothing to write down. A held target that is also mid-turn or limited is logged as that instead,
    # and its line is written by the next pulse: the look that held it has not changed either.
    #   A FLOORED TICK IS NOT RECORDED. It is not a repeat of anything the session was told — there is nothing
    # on the files to act on, which is why it is here — so an events line would be one more thing to read for
    # the sake of "still nothing". It rides the same channel as the back-off only to get PAST the tests above.
    if held or floored:
        if held and not r.dry and look and anything:
            ledger(target, note)
        r.log(target, "skip", why_due)
        return

    age = now() - last_tick(target)
    gap = conf("CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN", GAP_MIN) * 60
    if age < gap:
        r.log(target, "skip", f"ticked {int(age // 60)}m ago — one tick per cycle")
        return
    if r.dry:
        r.log(target, "tick", f"live and idle — would wake: {why_due}")
        return
    p = sh(f"{BIN}/cc-msg", target, (TICK if anything else TICK_GOALS).format(t=target, s=note), timeout=60)
    if p is not None and p.returncode == 0:
        # THE MARK IS THE WHOLE BACK-OFF, and it is written by EVERY tick, not just an empty one: it is what the
        # next run compares against to know whether it would be saying anything new. A look that has changed
        # never matches it and wakes at once.
        set_look(target, look)
        if (p.stdout or "").strip() == "queued":            # a dialog is up, or the pane went busy under us
            r.log(target, "tick", "queued — cc-msg holds it until the pane is free, and reports a dialog that stands")
        else:
            r.log(target, "tick", f"live and idle — woken: {why_due}")
    else:
        r.log(target, "report", f"tick failed: {why(p)}")


def main(argv):
    if argv and argv[0] == "selfcheck":
        return selfcheck()
    dry = listing = False
    only = []
    for a in argv:
        if a in ("--dry", "--dry-run"):
            dry = True
        elif a in ("--list", "-l"):
            listing = True
        elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__.strip())
            return 0
        elif a.startswith("-"):
            print(f"cc-pulse: unknown option {a}\nusage: cc-pulse [<target>…] [--dry-run] [--list] | selfcheck",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        else:
            only.append(a)
    targets = only or discover()
    bad = [t for t in targets if not NAME.fullmatch(t)]
    if bad:
        print(f"cc-pulse: not a target name: {' '.join(bad)} (<repo> or <repo>@<alias>)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if listing:
        print("\n".join(targets))
        return 0
    if conf("CC_PULSE", 1) == 0:                    # the kill switch: one line in ~/.cc/config stops the loop
        print("cc-pulse: CC_PULSE=0 in ~/.cc/config — the drive loop is off; nothing woken")
        return 0
    if not targets:
        print("cc-pulse: no boards and no live orchs — nothing to wake")
        return 0

    if not dry:                                     # a manual run during a timer run would double-tick everything
        try:                                        # (a dry run collides with nothing, and takes no lock: it is read-only)
            os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
            lock = open(f"{STATE}/pulse.lock", "w")
            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except OSError:
            print("cc-pulse: another pulse is running — this one stands down")
            return 0

    r = Run(dry)
    print(f"cc-pulse {stamp()}{' (dry run)' if dry else ''} — {len(targets)} target(s)")
    for t in targets:
        pulse(t, r)
    if not dry:
        # Belt and braces for the resume path. `cc-limit record` arms a one-shot at the reset; if that arming ever
        # fails, the record sits there and nobody is nudged at all. The sweep does nothing when nothing is due, and
        # it runs AFTER every tick so no session is sent two lines in one round.
        sh(f"{BIN}/cc-limit", "resume", timeout=120)
    r.trim()
    tally = " ".join(f"{v} {k}" for k, v in sorted(r.n.items())) or "nothing to do"
    print(f"cc-pulse: {tally}{' (dry run — nothing changed)' if dry else f' · log {LOG}'}")
    return 0


# ── selfcheck ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def selfcheck():
    """A whole fake box: its own HOME with two boards and one orch, stub tmux/cc/cc-msg/cc-handoff, and real
    processes standing in for the panes. Touches no tmux server, no board and no session on this machine, and
    every fixture name is a placeholder."""
    import contextlib
    import io
    import shutil
    import signal
    import tempfile
    global HOME, BOARDS, STATE, DEV, ORCHS, LOG, BIN
    p = f = 0

    def ok(name, cond):
        nonlocal p, f
        if cond:
            p += 1
        else:
            f += 1
            print(f"  FAIL {name}")

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as th:
        HOME, BIN = th, f"{th}/bin"
        BOARDS, STATE, DEV = f"{th}/.cc/boards", f"{th}/.cc/state", f"{th}/dev"
        ORCHS, LOG = f"{th}/.cc/slack/orchs.json", f"{th}/.cc/state/pulse.log"
        for d in (BIN, BOARDS, STATE, f"{th}/.cc/slack", f"{DEV}/alpha", f"{DEV}/beta"):
            os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        for name, body in (("tmux", FAKE_TMUX), ("cc", FAKE_CC), ("cc-msg", FAKE_MSG), ("cc-handoff", FAKE_HANDOFF),
                           ("cc-limit", FAKE_LIMIT), ("cc-scope", FAKE_SCOPE), ("cc-pause", FAKE_PAUSE),
                           ("cc-broker", FAKE_BROKER), ("cc-brief", FAKE_BRIEF),
                           ("cc-context", FAKE_CONTEXT), ("ps", FAKE_PS)):
            open(f"{BIN}/{name}", "w").write(body)
            os.chmod(f"{BIN}/{name}", 0o755)
        # a process whose name really is `claude`, for pgrep -x. A COPY OF BASH, not of sleep: this box's
        # coreutils is a multi-call binary that refuses to run under any other name.
        shutil.copy(os.path.realpath("/bin/bash"), f"{BIN}/claude")

        tmuxf, calls = f"{th}/tmux.txt", f"{th}/calls.txt"
        real_ps = shutil.which("ps") or "/usr/bin/ps"       # FAKE_PS's own fallback, before BIN goes on PATH
        os.environ.update(PATH=BIN + os.pathsep + os.environ.get("PATH", ""), CC_FAKE_TMUX=tmuxf,
                          CC_FAKE_CALLS=calls, CC_FAKE_BUSY="", CC_FAKE_DIALOG="", CC_FAKE_HANDOFF="",
                          CC_FAKE_HANDOFF_BROKEN="", CC_FAKE_LIMITED="", CC_FAKE_LIMIT_SEEN="",
                          CC_FAKE_LIMIT_STALE="", CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN="90", CC_PULSE="",
                          # the due-ness knobs are neutral for every case that is not about them: no asks, a
                          # working ledger, and the back-off OFF (CC_PULSE_QUIET=0) so a case that runs the same
                          # board twice is decided by the rule it is about and not by the same-look mark. The
                          # block at the end turns it on and is the only place that tests it.
                          CC_FAKE_ASKS="0", CC_FAKE_ASKS_OLD="0", CC_FAKE_SCOPE_BROKEN="",
                          CC_PULSE_QUIET="0", CC_FAKE_PAUSED="",
                          # …and the once-a-day floor OFF (CC_PULSE_IDLE_H=0), for the same reason: a case that
                          # runs the same board twice is decided by the rule it is about. The block at the end
                          # turns it on and is the only place that tests it.
                          CC_PULSE_IDLE_H="0",
                          # …and nothing could start, for every case that is not about that: cc-brief's answer
                          # when no queued row is free is no output at all, and the tick then carries no clause.
                          CC_FAKE_READY="", CC_FAKE_BRIEF_BROKEN="",
                          # …and cc-limit's resumed-episode file under $th, not the box's: a real limit that
                          # lifted this morning must not decide whether a mark in a fixture below is cold.
                          CC_LIMIT_RESUMED=f"{th}/limit-resumed",
                          # …and the never-started catch OFF for every case that is not about it: the fixture
                          # panes are seconds old, so a threshold in minutes would never fire anyway, but a
                          # negative one says so rather than leaving it to arithmetic.
                          CC_PULSE_STALL_MIN="-1", CC_FAKE_CTX="", CC_FAKE_CTX_BROKEN="",
                          # …and every real `ps` answered for real, except the one pid named here — pane_age's
                          # own tool-unwell case, off everywhere but the one block that tests it.
                          CC_REAL_PS=real_ps, CC_FAKE_PS_GONE="")

        def fixture(*cmd):                          # own group, no handle on our stdout (a stray child holds a pipe open)
            return subprocess.Popen(cmd, start_new_session=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                    stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)

        # `; true` twice over: without it bash execs the next command in its own place, and neither the pane
        # (which must have a child) nor the `claude` (which must not become `sleep`) would be what it claims.
        live = fixture("bash", "-c", f"{BIN}/claude -c 'sleep 300; true'; true")
        dead = fixture("sleep", "300")                            # a pane whose claude exited: a bare shell
        # the `cc-handoff --overlap` shape: pane -> bash -> claude, one level deeper than a direct-child test sees
        deep = fixture("bash", "-c", f"""bash -c "{BIN}/claude -c 'sleep 300; true'; true"; true""")
        work = fixture("bash", "-c", f"{BIN}/claude -p -c 'sleep 300; true'; true")   # headless: prints and exits
        # the shape `cc __runmain` leaves behind when its claude exits: `exec bash`, still a child of the pane
        shell = fixture("bash", "-c", "sleep 300; true")
        time.sleep(0.6)                                           # the live pane needs its child to exist first
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_NEWPID"] = str(live.pid)

        def board(repo, tracks):
            json.dump({"repo": repo, "tracks": tracks}, open(f"{BOARDS}/{repo}.json", "w"))

        # two boards and one orch, plus an archived orch that must never be a target. Each board carries one
        # queued row, so every case below is decided by the rule it is about and not by due-ness.
        board("alpha", {"t1": {"status": "queued"}})
        board("beta", {"t1": {"status": "queued"}})
        def bounded(fn, secs=10):   # a read that hangs on the pipe (a plain open would, for good) fails THIS case, not the suite
            import threading
            box = []
            def go():
                try: box.append(fn())
                except Exception as e: box.append(e)
            t = threading.Thread(target=go, daemon=True); t.start(); t.join(secs)
            return box[0] if box else "hung"
        os.mkfifo(f"{BOARDS}/pipe.json")   # A PIPE PLANTED WHERE A BOARD SHOULD BE (review of #224): the read is cc-board's opener now, so it is no board, never a hang
        ok("a pipe planted where a board should be is no rows to the tick, not a hang, and a real board still reads",
           bounded(lambda: board_rows("pipe")) == {} and board_rows("alpha") != {})
        os.remove(f"{BOARDS}/pipe.json")
        json.dump({"C1": {"target": "beta", "alias": "scout", "archived": False},
                   "C2": {"target": "beta", "alias": "old", "archived": True}}, open(ORCHS, "w"))
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n@2\tbeta@scout\t{live.pid}\n@3\talpha/aworker\t{dead.pid}\n")
        open(calls, "w").close()

        def did():
            return open(calls).read()

        def logged():
            try:
                return open(LOG).read()
            except OSError:
                return ""

        def since(prev):                            # only what the run just made — the log is cumulative
            return logged()[len(prev):]

        def run(*a):
            open(calls, "w").close()
            return main(list(a))

        tg = discover()
        ok("the targets are enumerated live — every board, plus every orch that is not archived",
           tg == ["alpha", "beta", "beta@scout"])
        ok("an ARCHIVED orch is retired, not asleep: it is no target", "beta@old" not in tg)

        # ---- --dry touches nothing at all
        run("--dry")
        ok("--dry starts no session and wakes nobody", "cc " not in did() and "msg " not in did())
        ok("--dry only reads: its one call, the limit test, is made dry as well",
           all(ln.startswith("limit record --dry ") for ln in did().splitlines()))
        ok("--dry writes no log, and no goals skeleton either",
           not os.path.exists(LOG) and not os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/alpha/goals.md"))

        # ---- (0) one run, three targets, each getting what its state deserves
        run()
        L = logged()
        ok("(0) one run covers the WHOLE machine: two boards and the orch, all three touched",
           all(t in L for t in ("alpha", "beta", "beta@scout")))
        ok("(0) …and a worker window is none of its business — it wakes sessions, not tracks",
           "alpha/aworker" not in L)
        ok("(1) a repo with no live session is started, idle", "cc alpha\n" in did() and "alpha start" in L)
        ok("(1) …and is NOT ticked in the same run: a session that just started has nothing to be woken from",
           "msg alpha " not in did())
        ok("(2) the live idle planning seat is woken, exactly once",
           did().count("msg beta ") == 1)
        ok("(2) …and the ORCH is not woken AT ALL: its own channel and its own workers are its loop",
           did().count("msg beta@scout ") == 0 and "an orch is not pulsed" in L)
        ok("the tick names the target and its goals file, and hands the judgement to the session",
           "independent engineer for beta" in did() and "~/.cc/state/beta/goals.md" in did()
           and "Nothing due = do nothing" in did())
        ok("(3) every REPO with no goals file got the skeleton, so no tick points at nothing",
           all("no goals recorded yet" in open(f"{STATE}/{t}/goals.md").read() for t in tg if "@" not in t))
        ok("(3) …and an orch gets none: a goals file for a target nothing ever points at is a file nobody reads",
           not os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/beta@scout/goals.md"))

        # ---- (1) the started session is live one cycle later, and is ticked then
        run()
        ok("(1) one pulse cycle after the start, a live idle session is there and gets its tick",
           "msg alpha " in did())
        ok("(2) NO PILEUP: the two woken seconds ago are skipped, not woken again",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "ticked 0m ago" in logged())

        # ---- an existing goals file belongs to the session
        open(f"{STATE}/beta/goals.md", "w").write("ship the thing\n")
        run()
        ok("a goals file that exists is never rewritten", open(f"{STATE}/beta/goals.md").read() == "ship the thing\n")

        # ---- (3) mid-handoff and mid-turn, with the gap out of the way
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n@2\tbeta@scout\t{live.pid}\n@3\talpha\t{live.pid}\n")
        os.environ.update(CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN="0", CC_FAKE_HANDOFF="beta", CC_FAKE_BUSY="@3")
        run()
        L = logged()
        ok("(3) a mid-handoff session is skipped — a tick would land on two sessions at once",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "beta skip — mid-handoff" in L)
        ok("(3) …and a session mid-turn is skipped too: its last tick is still unanswered",
           "msg alpha " not in did() and "mid-turn" in L)
        ok("(3) both skips are one log line each, and nothing else happens", "beta report" not in L)

        # ---- a session the usage limit stopped mid-turn
        os.environ.update(CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN="0", CC_FAKE_HANDOFF="", CC_FAKE_BUSY="", CC_FAKE_LIMITED="beta")
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("a session the usage limit stopped is handed to cc-limit, by the pane it is sitting on",
           "limit record @1 beta" in did())
        ok("…and is not ticked: the tick would meet the same limit, and cc-limit nudges it at the reset",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "usage limit stopped it mid-turn" in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_BUSY"] = "@1"
        run("beta")
        ok("a session mid-turn is never called interrupted — the limit test comes after the mid-turn one",
           "limit record" not in did())
        # exit 2 = cc-limit saw the limit line and did not record it (nothing corroborates it yet, or no reset time
        # has been named). The pane is limited either way, so the tick stays away — reading 2 as a plain failure is
        # what made USAGE.md's "the pulse does not tick a limited pane at all" a lie (review of #171).
        L1 = logged()
        os.environ.update(CC_FAKE_BUSY="", CC_FAKE_LIMITED="", CC_FAKE_LIMIT_SEEN="beta")
        run("beta")
        ok("a pane cc-limit has SEEN on the limit but not yet recorded is not ticked either",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "not recording it yet" in since(L1))
        os.environ.update(CC_FAKE_BUSY="", CC_FAKE_LIMITED="", CC_FAKE_LIMIT_SEEN="", CC_FAKE_LIMIT_STALE="beta")
        run("beta")
        ok("a limit line nothing corroborated for an hour is scrollback: cc-limit answers 1 and the pane ticks "
           "(review-171b)", "msg beta " in did())
        os.environ.update(CC_FAKE_BUSY="", CC_FAKE_LIMITED="", CC_FAKE_LIMIT_SEEN="", CC_FAKE_LIMIT_STALE="")
        run("beta")
        ok("…and with no limit on it that same session ticks as usual", "msg beta " in did())
        ok("every run ends by sweeping cc-limit's records, in case the one-shot at the reset never armed",
           did().rstrip().endswith("limit resume"))

        # ---- the gap is what stops a second tick, and it is configurable
        os.environ.update(CC_FAKE_HANDOFF="", CC_FAKE_BUSY="")
        run("beta")
        ok("with the gap at zero the same session ticks again — the gap alone is what held it",
           "msg beta " in did())
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN"] = "90"
        run("beta")
        ok("…and back at 90 min it does not", "msg beta " not in did())

        # ---- a board with no repo checked out anywhere
        open(f"{BOARDS}/delta.json", "w").write("{}\n")
        run()
        ok("a board with no ~/dev/<repo> is skipped, not started", "cc delta" not in did() and "nothing to start" in logged())

        # ---- an orch, dead or alive, is neither ticked nor started
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n")
        L0 = logged(); run("beta@scout")
        ok("an orch whose session is gone is not restarted either — a fresh one would mint a Slack channel",
           "cc beta" not in did() and "msg " not in did() and "an orch is not pulsed" in since(L0))

        # ---- it never wakes a pane with no interactive claude in it
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{dead.pid}\n")
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN"] = "0"
        run("beta")
        ok("a window whose claude has exited is a dead window: it is restarted, never typed at",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "cc beta\n" in did())

        # ---- the successor of an overlap handoff, whose claude is a GRANDCHILD of the pane
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{deep.pid}\n")
        run("beta")
        ok("an overlap-handoff successor is LIVE — its claude is a grandchild, and calling it dead would put a "
           "SECOND session on the repo", "msg beta " in did() and "cc beta\n" not in did())

        # ---- and the pane a subtree test must still refuse
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{work.pid}\n")
        run("beta")
        ok("a headless `claude -p` under the pane is not a session to type at",
           "msg beta " not in did())

        # ---- the window a dead session leaves behind, which `cc` will not replace
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{shell.pid}\n")
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("a leftover shell (pane still has a child) is not restartable by `cc`: the pulse says exactly that "
           "and claims no start it did not make",
           "cc beta\n" in did() and "msg beta " not in did()
           and "holds no session" in since(L0) and "beta start" not in since(L0))

        # ---- (3) a handoff record is open and the window reads DEAD — the branch that used to skip the check
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_HANDOFF"] = "beta"
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{dead.pid}\n")
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("(3) a record open + a dead window = NO START: a fresh un-gated session beside the successor is what "
           "kills a handoff, so the check comes before the start decision",
           "cc beta" not in did() and "mid-handoff" in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_HANDOFF"] = ""

        # ---- cc-handoff itself unwell: cheap things still happen, expensive ones do not
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_HANDOFF_BROKEN"] = "1"
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{dead.pid}\n")
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("cc-handoff unwell + a dead window = no start either: a tick is cheap to get wrong, a second session "
           "on a repo is not", "cc beta" not in did() and "not starting a second one blind" in since(L0))
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n")
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("…but a LIVE session is still ticked while cc-handoff is unwell — the box does not stop for one CLI",
           "msg beta " in did() and "ticking anyway" in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_HANDOFF_BROKEN"] = ""

        # ---- (4) a dialog is open: the tick is HANDED TO cc-msg, which answers it or queues behind it.
        # Until 2026-09-01 the pulse skipped here and the tick was gone — twelve sessions sat behind a model-limit
        # prompt and heard nothing all afternoon. The guard did not move, it moved INTO the one deliverer.
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_DIALOG"] = "@1"
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("(4) a pane with a dialog open is still ticked THROUGH cc-msg — it answers a known-safe prompt or "
           "queues the tick; the pulse never drops one on the floor",
           "msg beta " in did() and "queued" in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_DIALOG"] = ""

        # ---- the per-target opt-out
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n@2\talpha\t{live.pid}\n")
        open(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.off", "w").close()
        L0 = logged(); run("beta", "alpha")
        ok("a target with pulse.off is left alone, and only that one",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "pulse.off" in since(L0) and "msg alpha " in did())
        os.remove(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.off")

        # ---- the PROJECT pause. `cc-pause on beta` parks a whole project and nothing else — cc-pause is asked
        # about the target, and it reads the repo out of either shape. Its own fixture: the tmux table is written
        # here and CC_FAKE_PAUSED set and cleared around the run, so no later case inherits either.
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n@2\tbeta@scout\t{live.pid}\n@3\talpha\t{live.pid}\n")
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_PAUSED"] = "beta"
        L0 = logged(); run("beta", "beta@scout", "alpha")
        ok("a paused project is not ticked, and the log says the pause",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "beta skip — paused" in since(L0))
        ok("…and the pause is read for an orch too, so its line says the pause and not the orch rule — a person "
           "reading the log must see the louder fact", "beta@scout skip — paused" in since(L0))
        ok("…and another project pulses exactly as before: per project, never box-wide",
           "msg alpha " in did())
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_PAUSED"] = ""
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("…and the resumed project is ticked again on the very next run", "msg beta " in did())
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n@2\tbeta@scout\t{live.pid}\n")

        # ---- the kill switch
        os.environ["CC_PULSE"] = "0"
        L0 = logged(); rc = run("beta", "beta@scout")
        ok("CC_PULSE=0 stops the whole drive loop: nothing started, nobody woken, nothing logged",
           rc == 0 and did().strip() == "" and since(L0) == "")
        os.environ["CC_PULSE"] = ""

        # ---- usage
        ok("a target name that is not one is a usage error, never a guess", main(["../etc/passwd"]) == 2)
        ok("an unknown option is a usage error", main(["--nope"]) == 2)
        ok("--list says what a run would cover and does nothing", main(["--list"]) == 0)

        # ---- DUE-NESS: who is worth a wake, and what the wake carries
        os.environ.update(CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN="0", CC_FAKE_HANDOFF="", CC_FAKE_BUSY="", CC_FAKE_DIALOG="")
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n")

        def decide(tracks, asks="0", old="0"):
            """One due-ness case: that board, that many open asks (`old` of them past their line), one run on
            beta, no same-look mark left over."""
            board("beta", tracks)
            os.environ.update(CC_FAKE_ASKS=asks, CC_FAKE_ASKS_OLD=old)
            if os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look"):
                os.remove(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look")
            was = logged()
            run("beta")
            return did(), since(was)

        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}})
        ok("THE DRIVE STAYS: a quiet board is still woken — a session that runs only when a row is queued never "
           "lifts its head to its goals", "msg beta " in d and "the drive tick" in L)
        ok("…and that tick says so, off the files, so an empty check is one cheap turn",
           "no red main, no open ask, no row queued or in flight" in d)
        ok("…and the quiet tick is the GOALS tick: it says to work goals and never says to do nothing — the "
           "back-off mark would turn that clause into a 6 h no-op",
           "goals tick" in d and "move one of them" in d and "do nothing" not in d)

        d, L = decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}})
        ok("a main-gate row IS red main, and the tick carries it", "msg beta " in d and "main-gate row RED" in d)

        d, L = decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "merged"}})
        ok("…and a main-gate row that is merged is history, not a red main", "main-gate row RED" not in d)

        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}}, asks="2")
        ok("the tick counts the owner's open asks for the session", "2 owner asks open" in d)

        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued"}, "t2": {"status": "blocked"}, "t3": {"status": "running"}})
        ok("…and the rows that are queued or blocked, by name", "2 rows queued/blocked (t1, t2)" in d)
        ok("…and the rows already in flight, with the status each is in", "1 row in flight (t3 running)" in d)
        ok("a count of queued rows says nothing about ROOM, so the tick makes no such claim on its own",
           "could start now" not in d)

        # WHICH OF THOSE COULD ACTUALLY BE STARTED is cc-brief's arithmetic — the board against git against each
        # brief — and the tick carries its headline. Both directions, because the useful half of a report that
        # only speaks up when there is something to say is that it is silent the rest of the time.
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_READY"] = "beta: 2 of 3 queued rows could start now — 1 group shares a file"
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued"}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}, "t3": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("the tick says which of the queued rows could be STARTED right now, and where the rest of it is",
           "2 of 3 queued rows could start now" in d and "1 group shares a file" in d and "cc-brief ready beta" in d)
        ok("…and the repo is not named twice — the tick already says whose board it is", "beta: 2 of 3" not in d)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_READY"] = ""
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued"}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}, "t3": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("…and NOTHING is said when nothing could start: no clause, no count, no pointer",
           "could start now" not in d and "cc-brief ready" not in d and "3 rows queued/blocked" in d)

        # A ROW A LIVE SESSION IS DOING IN ITS OWN SUBAGENTS is work in flight, not work waiting to be picked up.
        # Nothing on the box holds its worktree — no worker, no cc-loop, no window of its own — so `queued` read
        # as free and every tick offered it for a second dispatch (owner, 2026-09-04: backlog rows run as the
        # planning session's subagents). The mark is that session's own tmux window, and it is worth exactly what
        # that window is worth: every case below is one way for it to be worth nothing.
        FRESH = stamp()
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued", "agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": FRESH}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("a queued row a LIVE session marked as its subagent's work is not offered as queued — and the "
           "unmarked one beside it still is",
           "1 row queued/blocked (t2)" in d and "1 row in flight (t1 queued (a subagent has it))" in d)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued", "agent": "@9:777", "agent_at": FRESH}})
        ok("…a mark whose window is gone is worth nothing: the row is offered again, exactly as before this",
           "1 row queued/blocked (t1)" in d)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued", "agent": "@1:778", "agent_at": FRESH}})
        ok("…so is one carrying another tmux server's number — window ids restart at @0 when the server does, "
           "and a mark left by a session that died in a reboot must not match whatever is @1 now",
           "1 row queued/blocked (t1)" in d)
        # A ROW A PERSON IS DELIBERATELY SITTING ON is the same failure from the other end (cc-board's `held`):
        # `queued` is all an undispatched row can say, so a row somebody stopped on purpose was offered for
        # dispatch every tick. Unlike a mark, a hold expires on nothing and no window can be gone from under it
        # — a person put it there and a person lifts it — so the two cases are the word on and the word off.
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued", "held": "behind #309, which carries a paid verdict"},
                       "t2": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("a held row is not offered for dispatch — and the unheld one beside it still is",
           "1 row queued/blocked (t2)" in d and "1 row in flight (t1 queued (on hold))" in d)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued", "held": ""}, "t2": {"status": "queued", "held": "   "}})
        ok("…and it takes a REASON to hold one: an empty word, or one that is only spaces, holds nothing",
           "2 rows queued/blocked (t1, t2)" in d)
        # …and the rest of the rule, on agent_held itself. Two windows of one name would confuse `windows()`,
        # which is a different question from this one, so these drive the test rather than a whole run.
        ok("agent_held reads a live mark, and only for the repo whose window it is: A SESSION SPEAKS FOR ITS "
           "OWN REPO — this board is one file every session can write, and a mark from outside must not hold "
           "a row of it open", agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": FRESH}}, "beta") == {"t1"}
           and agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": FRESH}}, "alpha") == set())
        # …AND AN ORCHESTRATOR SPEAKS FOR ITS REPO TOO. It sits in the same checkout on the same board, in a
        # window named `<repo>@<alias>`; read as plain equality every row it marked was offered again
        # underneath it, and a handoff carrying marks to an orch successor carried them where they stopped
        # counting. Still not another repo's, which is the half this test has always been about.
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta@dash\t{live.pid}\n")
        ok("an orchestrator's window holds its own repo's row, and no other repo's",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": FRESH}}, "beta") == {"t1"}
           and agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": FRESH}}, "bet") == set()
           and agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": FRESH}}, "alpha") == set())
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{dead.pid}\n")
        ok("…and a window whose session has exited holds nothing: `cc` leaves the window behind (exec bash), "
           "so the mark says a SESSION is in it, never that the window exists",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": FRESH}}, "beta") == set())
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n")
        body = open(f"{BIN}/tmux").read()
        open(f"{BIN}/tmux", "w").write("#!/usr/bin/env bash\nexit 1\n")
        ok("…and a tmux that will not answer holds NOTHING: the row goes back to being offered, which is what "
           "this file did before the mark existed — a listing that failed must never park work silently",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": FRESH}}, "beta") == set())
        open(f"{BIN}/tmux", "w").write(body)
        ok("…while a board with no mark on it asks tmux nothing at all",
           agent_held({"t1": {"status": "queued"}}, "beta") == set())
        # ALIVE IS NOT WORKING, the same rule cc-reconcile makes: a session marks a row, its subagent dies or it
        # moves on without clearing the mark, and the window lives on — the row would sit in flight for ever,
        # offered to nobody, with nothing on the box able to see that no work is happening on it.
        ok("A COLD MARK HOLDS NOTHING: one made 7 h ago on a window whose session is still very much alive is "
           "past the bound, so the row is offered again",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 7 * 3600)}}, "beta") == set())
        ok("…and one made a minute ago on the same window still holds it — the bound expires a mark, it does "
           "not abolish it, and a session still on the row refreshes the time by marking again",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 60)}}, "beta") == {"t1"})
        ok("…and a mark with no time on it at all holds nothing, which is every mark written before this: "
           "unreadable is 'long ago' here as everywhere else in this file",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777"}}, "beta") == set())

        # …AND A USAGE LIMIT ENDS A MARK BEFORE ITS TIME (2026-09-05): the limit killed the subagents mid-row and
        # their marks stayed warm for hours after it lifted, so rows nobody was doing were offered to nobody.
        # Own fixture: the file is written here and removed again, so every case above and below reads no episode.
        # "<started>\t<reset>\t<resumed>": an episode that BEGAN an hour ago and reset ten minutes ago.
        rezf = os.environ["CC_LIMIT_RESUMED"]
        open(rezf, "w").write(f"{int(now() - 3600)}\t{int(now() - 600)}\t{int(now() - 590)}\n")
        ok("a mark made BEFORE a limit that has since lifted is cold at any age — the subagent holding it died "
           "on the 429 — so the row is offered again instead of sitting in flight until the 6 h bound",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 5400)}}, "beta") == set())
        # THE BAR IS THE START, NOT THE RESET (review of #264). cc-model moves the box to the fallback about a
        # minute into an episode and it goes on working, so a mark made between the start and the reset is a
        # subagent that is alive: judging by the reset offered a row that was in flight.
        ok("…while a mark made DURING the episode, after the box switched to the fallback, holds its row: the "
           "box did not stop, and that subagent is still working",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 1800)}}, "beta") == {"t1"})
        ok("…and one made after the box came back holds it on its own age, exactly as before: the episode moves "
           "the bound, it does not abolish the mark",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 300)}}, "beta") == {"t1"})
        open(rezf, "w").write(f"{int(now() - 3600)}\t{int(now() + 3600)}\t{int(now())}\n")   # reset an hour out
        ok("…and an episode whose reset has NOT passed decides nothing: while the limit stands, the subagents "
           "that are out are not dead yet and their rows are still theirs",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 5400)}}, "beta") == {"t1"})
        open(rezf, "w").write(f"{int(now() - 3600)}\t{int(now() - 600)}\n")   # two fields: not what cc-limit writes
        ok("…and a file that is not the three fields cc-limit writes says nothing at all, rather than reading "
           "some other field as the start",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 5400)}}, "beta") == {"t1"})
        os.unlink(rezf)
        ok("…and with no episode on file the age bound is the whole rule, which is what this file did before",
           agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 5400)}}, "beta") == {"t1"}
           and agent_held({"t1": {"agent": "@1:777", "agent_at": stamp(now() - 7 * 3600)}}, "beta") == set())

        # A ROW IN `review` IS A PR NOBODY HAS LANDED. Read as nothing-due it cost the worst wake of the lot:
        # the session was told "goals are the work" and then muted for 6 h with its own PR still open.
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "review"}})
        ok("a PR waiting to land is not a quiet board: the tick carries it and does NOT send the session to goals",
           "msg beta " in d and "1 row in flight (t1 review)" in d and "goals tick" not in d)

        os.environ["CC_FAKE_SCOPE_BROKEN"] = "1"
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}})
        ok("a ledger that cannot answer is said out loud, never read as 'nothing open'",
           "msg beta " in d and "could not count the open asks" in d)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_SCOPE_BROKEN"] = ""

        d, L = decide({"s1": {"status": "running", "kind": "session"},
                       "s2": {"status": "queued", "kind": "session"}})
        ok("a board of nothing but session rows is a channel/course repo: NEVER pulsed, and the log says why",
           "msg beta " not in d and "kind=session" in L)
        d, L = decide({"s1": {"status": "running", "kind": "session"}}, asks="3")
        ok("…never pulsed even with owner asks open — that channel is its own loop, and this one does not drive it",
           "msg beta " not in d)
        d, L = decide({"s1": {"status": "running", "kind": "session"}, "t1": {"status": "running"}})
        ok("one track row among them and it is an ordinary repo again", "msg beta " in d)

        # ---- THE SAME LOOK: the first time a look is sent it is worth a turn; the same one two hours later is
        # not. Measured 2026-09-05 — a session with one PR parked in `review` paid a full-context wake every 2 h
        # to answer "unchanged, no action": 12 turns and $51 in a day it did no work.
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_QUIET"] = "6"
        d, L = decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}})
        ok("every tick writes down the look it carried, not just an empty one",
           "msg beta " in d and os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look"))
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("…and the next pulse with that same look does not wake the session at all, with the reason in the log",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "same look" in since(L0))
        ok("…and the held tick is WRITTEN DOWN, once, where it wakes nobody — cc-broker carries it out on the "
           "next message the session gets for a real reason",
           "broker ledger --once beta pulse: main-gate row RED · 2 rows in flight (main-gate running, "
           "t1 review)" in did())

        os.truncate(calls, 0); run("--dry-run", "beta")
        ok("…and a HELD tick on a dry run writes nothing at all — no ledger line, the same as no tick",
           "broker " not in did())

        # THE BACK-OFF MUST NOT SWALLOW THE LIMIT TEST. `limit_state()` is the only caller of `cc-limit record`
        # for an interactive session, and a limited session's board does not move, so its look never changes:
        # held above that call, the one-shot that resumes its interrupted turn would never be armed and the turn
        # would be lost for the whole window and then for good (review of #246).
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_LIMITED"] = "beta"
        L0 = logged(); os.truncate(calls, 0); run("beta")
        ok("a HELD session the usage limit stopped is STILL handed to cc-limit, so its resume one-shot is armed",
           "limit record @1 beta" in did())
        ok("…and it is logged as limited, not as a repeat, and no ledger line is written for it",
           "msg beta " not in did() and "broker " not in did()
           and "usage limit stopped it mid-turn" in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_LIMITED"] = ""

        board("beta", {MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}})
        L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("a look that CHANGED breaks the window at once — the mark holds a repeat, it does not mute a session",
           "msg beta " in did() and "1 row queued/blocked (t2)" in did())

        # A ROW'S WORD IS PART OF THE LOOK. The note prints queued/blocked rows bare, so `queued` → `blocked`
        # shares a note with what came before it — and that is a row that STOPPED.
        decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "queued"}}); os.truncate(calls, 0)
        board("beta", {MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "blocked"}})
        run("beta")
        ok("a queued row that went blocked is a change, not a repeat — the note reads the same and the mark "
           "must not", "msg beta " in did())

        # THE BACK-OFF IS A BACK-OFF FROM REPEATING OURSELVES, so it needs somebody who heard us the first time.
        decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}})     # a live beta, ticked: the mark is set
        open(tmuxf, "w").write("")                              # …and now its session is gone
        os.truncate(calls, 0); L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("a target whose session DIED is started even though the mark says it heard this — the back-off must "
           "never be what leaves a repo with an open PR unstarted for a day",
           "cc beta" in did() and "start" in since(L0))
        ok("…and starting one clears the mark, because the session that heard the last tick is not there any "
           "more", not os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look"))

        decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}})
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_HANDOFF"] = "beta"
        os.truncate(calls, 0); run("beta")
        ok("…and a successor mid-handoff is not muted by what its predecessor was told: the mark goes with the "
           "handoff", "msg beta " not in did() and not os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look"))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_HANDOFF"] = ""
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n")

        d, L = decide({"s1": {"status": "running", "kind": "session"}})
        ok("a channel repo is not held, it is never pulsed — and nothing is written to an events file it has no "
           "use for", "msg beta " not in d and "broker " not in d and "kind=session" in L)

        # The note truncates to three row names so the tick stays one line; the mark must not, or a board whose
        # fourth row moved would read as unchanged and the session would never hear about it.
        board("beta", {MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}, "t2": {"status": "review"},
                       "t3": {"status": "review"}, "t4": {"status": "review"}})
        run("beta"); os.truncate(calls, 0)
        board("beta", {MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}, "t2": {"status": "review"},
                       "t3": {"status": "review"}, "t4": {"status": "running"}})
        run("beta")
        ok("a row that moved BEYOND the three the tick names still counts as a change — the mark is the whole "
           "board state, never the sentence the session reads", "msg beta " in did())

        decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}})     # a fresh mark, then age it past the window
        t = now() - 7 * 3600
        os.utime(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look", (t, t))
        os.truncate(calls, 0); run("beta")
        ok("past CC_PULSE_QUIET the session gets that look again — a back-off, not silence", "msg beta " in did())

        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}})
        ok("nothing at all on the files is the GOALS tick — the look mark is not what holds THAT path, the "
           "once-a-day floor is (its own block below)", "msg beta " in d and "goals tick" in d)

        decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}})
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_QUIET"] = "0"
        os.truncate(calls, 0); run("beta")
        ok("CC_PULSE_QUIET=0 turns the back-off off entirely: every pulse looks, the way it did before this",
           "msg beta " in did())
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_QUIET"] = "6"

        # ---- A SESSION THAT NEVER STARTED. The launch hands a session its purpose as the first turn; when that
        # turn is lost the window still looks healthy, and until 2026-09-08 nothing on the box said so.
        # A PLANNING SEAT is the fixture: an orch takes no tick at all now, so this catch is the seats'.
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_QUIET"] = "0"
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_STALL_MIN"] = "0"          # the fixture panes are seconds old; the AGE bound is its own case below
        os.environ.update(CC_FAKE_BUSY="", CC_FAKE_DIALOG="", CC_FAKE_HANDOFF="", CC_FAKE_HANDOFF_BROKEN="",
                          CC_FAKE_LIMITED="", CC_FAKE_LIMIT_SEEN="", CC_FAKE_CTX="", CC_FAKE_CTX_BROKEN="")
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n")                        # earlier cases rewrote it
        board("beta", {"t1": {"status": "queued"}})
        L0 = logged()
        run("beta")
        ok("a live session with no record of ever finishing a turn is NAMED — with its address, so the finding "
           "says which pane to look at", "beta report" in since(L0) and "never finished a turn" in since(L0))
        ok("…and it is still ticked: the finding is the news, the tick is the recovery this file already had",
           "msg beta " in did())
        # THE CONTROL, and it is the one that matters: a session that HAS taken a turn must never be named. Age 0
        # is a record written since that pane started, which is exactly what "it got going" looks like.
        L0 = logged()
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_CTX"] = "beta\t-\t12\t120000\t1000000\tprocess\t0\t-\t-\n"
        run("beta")
        ok("control: a session cc-context has a record for since its pane started is working, and is not named",
           "never finished a turn" not in since(L0))
        # …and a record from BEFORE this pane is a previous session's in the same window, not this one's.
        L0 = logged()
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_CTX"] = "beta\t-\t12\t120000\t1000000\tprocess\t99999\t-\t-\n"
        run("beta")
        ok("…and a record older than the pane belongs to whatever ran there before: still never started",
           "never finished a turn" in since(L0))
        # A TRACK'S ROW IS NOT ITS REPO'S. Column two carries the track, and reading it as the session a person
        # steers would call a repo started because one of its workers had taken a turn.
        L0 = logged()
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_CTX"] = "beta\tsomework\t12\t120000\t1000000\tprocess\t0\t-\t-\n"
        run("beta")
        ok("control: a TRACK's record is not the session's — column two is read, not ignored",
           "never finished a turn" in since(L0))
        # NEVER CRY WOLF OVER A TOOL THAT CANNOT ANSWER.
        L0 = logged()
        os.environ.update(CC_FAKE_CTX="", CC_FAKE_CTX_BROKEN="1")
        run("beta")
        ok("cc-context unable to answer is not a finding — a sick tool must not report a healthy session",
           "never finished a turn" not in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_CTX_BROKEN"] = ""
        # NOR A ps THAT CANNOT ANSWER. pane_age's own ps call can fail the same way — the pid raced or exited
        # between the tmux snapshot and this call — and that must be just as quiet; nothing tested it before now.
        L0 = logged()
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_PS_GONE"] = str(live.pid)
        run("beta")
        ok("pane_age's own ps failing (a bogus or gone pid) is not a finding either — same rule as a tool that "
           "cannot answer", "never finished a turn" not in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_PS_GONE"] = ""
        # …AND NOT BEFORE ITS TIME. The whole point of the bound is that a session mid-first-turn is thinking.
        L0 = logged()
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_STALL_MIN"] = "600"
        run("beta")
        ok("control: under the stall bound a session with no turn yet is simply young, and is not named",
           "never finished a turn" not in since(L0))
        L0 = logged()
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_STALL_MIN"] = "-1"
        run("beta")
        ok("control: a negative bound turns the catch off entirely", "never finished a turn" not in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_QUIET"] = "6"

        # ---- A TICK GOES WHERE THE SEAT CAN ACT, AND NOWHERE ELSE. Measured over 2026-09-02..08: pulse
        # wakes cost $589, $91 a day, and the seat's own answer to most of them was "nothing due, reply
        # nothing" (owner, 2026-09-08: "make curbing spend a number one priority"). The floor is read off the
        # LOG — the last tick — so every case here writes the log it is deciding against and owns it.
        def ticked(mins):
            """The log this target would have if it had last been ticked `mins` ago, and nothing else — plus
            cc-brief back to silent, so a case that wants an answer out of it says so itself."""
            open(LOG, "w").write(f"{stamp(now() - mins * 60)} beta tick — a look-up {mins}m ago\n")
            os.environ["CC_FAKE_READY"] = ""

        os.environ.update(CC_FAKE_READY="", CC_PULSE_IDLE_H="6")
        ticked(10)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued"}, "t2": {"status": "review"}}, asks="2", old="0")
        ok("(1) green main, a queued row nothing can start, asks inside their line: NOBODY is woken",
           "msg beta " not in d)
        ok("(1) …and the one line it writes says what it saw and why it woke nobody",
           "nothing to act on" in L and "1 row queued/blocked (t1)" in L and "2 owner asks open" in L
           and "1 row in flight (t2 review)" in L)
        ok("(1) …and it dispatches nothing, starts nothing and writes no events line for it",
           "cc " not in d and "broker " not in d)

        # (2) EACH of the three things a seat can act on, and beside each one the control that differs only in
        # that thing. The payload is today's, unchanged — the wake is what moved, not the sentence.
        ticked(10)
        d, L = decide({MAIN_GATE: {"status": "running"}, "t1": {"status": "review"}})
        ok("(2) a RED main wakes the seat inside the round, carrying the payload it always did",
           "msg beta " in d and "main-gate row RED" in d and "t1 review" in d)

        ticked(10)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_READY"] = "beta: 1 of 2 queued rows could start now"
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued"}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("(2) a queued row that could actually be STARTED wakes it — a count of queued rows never could",
           "msg beta " in d and "1 of 2 queued rows could start now" in d)
        ticked(10)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_READY"] = ("beta: 2 queued rows, none can be called free — a row is in flight and "
                                       "nothing places it")
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued"}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("(2) …and an answer cc-brief WITHHELD wakes it too: giving that row a branch is the work",
           "msg beta " in d and "none can be called free" in d)
        ticked(10)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_READY"] = ""
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued"}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("(2) control: the same two queued rows with nothing able to start them wake nobody",
           "msg beta " not in d and "nothing to act on" in L)
        # …AND A cc-brief THAT CRASHED IS NOT THAT CONTROL. Its non-zero exit was read as an empty answer, so a
        # broken tool read as "no room" and the queued rows waited on the daily floor with nobody told (review
        # of #390). The timeout beside it has always been unread; a crash is the same fact.
        ticked(10)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_BRIEF_BROKEN"] = "1"
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "queued"}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("(2) …and a cc-brief that CRASHED wakes the seat: a tool that cannot answer is not a tool saying "
           "there is no room", "msg beta " in d and "is unread this tick" in d and "no such board" in d)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_BRIEF_BROKEN"] = ""

        ticked(10)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "blocked"}})
        ok("(2) a row that STOPPED wakes the seat — `blocked` is what a landing that died leaves behind, and "
           "unsticking it is a decision only the seat takes",
           "msg beta " in d and "1 row queued/blocked (t1)" in d)
        ticked(10)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "blocked", "held": "behind #309, which carries a paid verdict"}})
        ok("(2) control: the same row a PERSON is deliberately sitting on is not a stop to unstick — a hold is "
           "lifted by the person who set it, and a wake cannot do it",
           "msg beta " not in d and "nothing to act on" in L)

        ticked(10)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}}, asks="1", old="1")
        ok("(2) an owner ask that has waited PAST ITS LINE wakes the seat",
           "msg beta " in d and "1 owner ask open" in d)
        ticked(10)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}}, asks="1", old="0")
        ok("(2) control: the same ask INSIDE its line wakes nobody — the session read the message it was made "
           "in, so the pulse is not how it hears of it", "msg beta " not in d)
        ticked(10)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_SCOPE_BROKEN"] = "1"
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}})
        ok("(2) …and a ledger that CANNOT ANSWER wakes it: that is not a ledger saying 'nothing open'",
           "msg beta " in d and "could not count the open asks" in d)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_SCOPE_BROKEN"] = ""

        # (4) …and a repo with nothing to act on is still looked at, once a round.
        ticked(10)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}})
        ok("(4) a repo with no live row and no open ask, looked at ten minutes ago, is not looked at again",
           "msg beta " not in d and "inside the 6h round" in L and "broker " not in d)
        ticked(7 * 60)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}})
        ok("(4) …and past the round it is: a floor, not silence — this is the wake that lifts the session's "
           "head to its goals", "msg beta " in d and "goals tick" in d)
        ticked(7 * 60)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "review"}, "t2": {"status": "queued"}})
        ok("(4) …and the daily look-up for a board that is NOT empty carries what the files say, not the goals "
           "line", "msg beta " in d and "1 row in flight (t1 review)" in d and "goals tick" not in d)

        # (4) THE ROUND HOLDS A WAKE AND NOTHING ELSE. It was taken at the cheap gate, above the liveness and
        # limit tests, so inside the round a seat that had died stayed dead for up to CC_PULSE_IDLE_H and a
        # limit-stopped one never reached `cc-limit record` — the #246 loss again (review of #390).
        ticked(10)
        board("beta", {"t1": {"status": "merged"}})         # nothing to act on: the round is what holds the tick
        open(tmuxf, "w").write("")                          # …and the seat is not there at all any more
        os.truncate(calls, 0); L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("(4) a seat that DIED inside the round is still started — the floor holds a wake, never a restart",
           "cc beta" in did() and "msg beta " not in did() and "beta start" in since(L0))
        open(tmuxf, "w").write(f"@1\tbeta\t{live.pid}\n")

        ticked(10)
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_LIMITED"] = "beta"
        board("beta", {"t1": {"status": "merged"}})
        os.truncate(calls, 0); L0 = logged(); run("beta")
        ok("(4) …and a seat the usage limit stopped inside the round is still handed to cc-limit, so the "
           "one-shot that resumes its interrupted turn is armed",
           "limit record @1 beta" in did() and "msg beta " not in did()
           and "usage limit stopped it mid-turn" in since(L0))
        os.environ["CC_FAKE_LIMITED"] = ""
        ticked(10)
        d, L = decide({"t1": {"status": "merged"}})
        ok("(4) …and a floored tick writes NO events line: it repeats nothing the session was told, so there is "
           "nothing for cc-broker to carry out", "broker " not in d and "inside the 6h round" in L)
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_IDLE_H"] = "0"

        # (3) THE ORCH, one last time and from the dry run: a verdict per target, and its reason, touching nothing.
        buf = io.StringIO()
        os.truncate(calls, 0)
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
            rc = main(["--dry", "beta", "beta@scout"])
        out = buf.getvalue()
        # Read it PER TARGET and not as one blob: `"beta" in out` is satisfied by the orch's own line, so a
        # seat that had silently stopped being printed would have passed. Every verdict line is
        # "  <target padded> <verb> <reason>", so the first word is the target it is about.
        verdict = {ln.split()[0]: ln for ln in out.splitlines() if ln.startswith("  ")}
        ok("(3) --dry prints a verdict for EVERY target, the orch among them, with the reason beside it",
           rc == 0 and "skip" in verdict.get("beta@scout", "")
           and "an orch is not pulsed" in verdict.get("beta@scout", "")
           and "would wake" in verdict.get("beta", "") and "msg " not in did())

        # ---- --dry-run: the whole verdict, in writing, having touched nothing
        board("beta", {"t1": {"status": "queued"}})
        if os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look"):
            os.remove(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look")
        L0 = logged()
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
            rc = run("--dry-run", "beta")
        ok("--dry-run names who would be woken and what the files say, and writes nothing at all — no tick, no "
           "log, no mark, and the one call it does make (the limit test) is made dry too",
           rc == 0 and "would wake" in buf.getvalue() and "1 row queued/blocked (t1)" in buf.getvalue()
           and all(ln.startswith("limit record --dry ") for ln in did().splitlines())
           and since(L0) == "" and not os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/beta/pulse.look"))

        # ---- the log is the record, and it is bounded
        os.environ["CC_PULSE_GAP_MIN"] = "90"
        open(LOG, "w").write("x\n" * (LOG_MAX + 50))
        run("beta")
        ok("the log is trimmed to its cap — a 2 h timer must not grow a file forever",
           len(open(LOG).read().splitlines()) <= LOG_MAX)

        for proc in (live, dead, deep, work, shell):   # the group, so the sleep under the live shell goes too
            try:
                os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                pass
            proc.wait(timeout=10)

    print(f"cc-pulse selfcheck: {p} passed, {f} failed")
    return 1 if f else 0


FAKE_TMUX = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub: windows are "id<TAB>name<TAB>pane_pid" lines in $CC_FAKE_TMUX, and capture-pane shows Claude's
# working footer for the window ids in $CC_FAKE_BUSY, a permission dialog for those in $CC_FAKE_DIALOG (the real
# wording, which carries NEITHER of the other two markers), and an idle prompt for every other.
set -u; F="$CC_FAKE_TMUX"; cmd="${1:-}"; shift || true
fmt=""; targ=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do case "$1" in -F) fmt="${2:-}"; shift 2;; -t) targ="${2:-}"; shift 2;; *) shift;; esac; done
case "$cmd" in
  list-windows)
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r id name pid; do
      [ -n "${id:-}" ] || continue
      s="$fmt"; s="${s//\#\{window_id\}/$id}"; s="${s//\#\{window_name\}/$name}"; s="${s//\#\{pane_pid\}/$pid}"
      s="${s//\#\{pid\}/${CC_FAKE_TMUX_SRV:-777}}"      # the tmux SERVER's pid: half of an `agent` mark
      printf '%s\n' "$s"
    done < "$F";;
  capture-pane)
    case " ${CC_FAKE_DIALOG:-} " in *" $targ "*) printf 'Do you want to proceed?\n 1. Yes\n'; exit 0;; esac
    case " ${CC_FAKE_BUSY:-} " in *" $targ "*) echo "esc to interrupt";; *) echo "> ";; esac;;
esac
exit 0
"""

FAKE_CC = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub for `cc <repo>`, and it does what the real one does, not what would be convenient: `cc` kills an
# existing window only when its pane has NO child, and creates one only when there is no window by that name. A
# stub that always produced a live window would hide the case the real `cc` cannot fix — a pane whose claude has
# exited but whose fallback shell is still a child of it — and the pulse would claim a start that never happened.
set -u
printf 'cc %s\n' "$*" >> "$CC_FAKE_CALLS"
pid=$(awk -F'\t' -v n="$1" '$2==n{print $3; exit}' "$CC_FAKE_TMUX")
if [ -n "$pid" ]; then
  pgrep -P "$pid" >/dev/null 2>&1 && exit 0                  # pane_live: cc leaves the window exactly as it is
  awk -F'\t' -v n="$1" '$2!=n' "$CC_FAKE_TMUX" > "$CC_FAKE_TMUX.tmp" && mv "$CC_FAKE_TMUX.tmp" "$CC_FAKE_TMUX"
fi
printf '@%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$(( $(wc -l < "$CC_FAKE_TMUX") + 90 ))" "$1" "$CC_FAKE_NEWPID" >> "$CC_FAKE_TMUX"
"""

FAKE_MSG = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub for `cc-msg <window> <text>`: records who was typed at, and what. Speaks the real contract —
# with a dialog up on that window it prints "queued" (rc 0), because cc-msg spools instead of dropping.
set -u
printf 'msg %s :: %s\n' "${1:-}" "${2:-}" >> "$CC_FAKE_CALLS"
case " ${CC_FAKE_DIALOG:-} " in *" @1 "*) [ "${1:-}" = beta ] && echo queued;; esac
exit 0
"""

FAKE_LIMIT = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub for `cc-limit record [--dry] <window-id> <target>`, and it answers with the real thing's three
# codes: 0 when that target is in $CC_FAKE_LIMITED (recorded), 2 when it is in $CC_FAKE_LIMIT_SEEN (the limit line
# is there, not recorded yet), 1 otherwise — including $CC_FAKE_LIMIT_STALE, a limit line nothing corroborated for
# an hour, which the real thing reads as scrollback. Records the call, so --dry can be shown to stay dry.
set -u
printf 'limit %s\n' "$*" >> "$CC_FAKE_CALLS"
tgt=""; for a in "$@"; do tgt="$a"; done          # the target is the last argument
case " ${CC_FAKE_LIMITED:-} " in *" $tgt "*) exit 0;; esac
case " ${CC_FAKE_LIMIT_SEEN:-} " in *" $tgt "*) exit 2;; esac
case " ${CC_FAKE_LIMIT_STALE:-} " in *" $tgt "*) exit 1;; esac
exit 1
"""

FAKE_SCOPE = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub for `cc-scope list <repo> --json`: the array the real one prints for the UNSETTLED asks —
# $CC_FAKE_ASKS of them, of which the first $CC_FAKE_ASKS_OLD were asked long ago and the rest a minute back.
# Exits 2 when $CC_FAKE_SCOPE_BROKEN is set: the ledger is there and cannot answer.
set -u
[ -n "${CC_FAKE_SCOPE_BROKEN:-}" ] && exit 2
n="${CC_FAKE_ASKS:-0}"; old="${CC_FAKE_ASKS_OLD:-0}"
fresh=$(date -u -d '1 minute ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
printf '['
i=0
while [ "$i" -lt "$n" ]; do
  [ "$i" -gt 0 ] && printf ','
  if [ "$i" -lt "$old" ]; then at=2000-01-01T00:00:00Z; else at="$fresh"; fi
  printf '{"id":"a%s","status":"open","asked":"%s"}' "$i" "$at"
  i=$((i+1))
done
printf ']\n'
"""

FAKE_BRIEF = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub for `cc-brief ready <repo> <row>…`: prints $CC_FAKE_READY, and prints nothing when it is empty —
# which is the real one's answer when no queued row could be started. Exit 0 either way: it reports, never gates.
# $CC_FAKE_BRIEF_BROKEN is the tool DEAD ON ITS FEET — a non-zero exit with a word about it on stderr, which is
# not the same fact as "no queued row could start" and must never be read as one.
set -u
if [ -n "${CC_FAKE_BRIEF_BROKEN:-}" ]; then echo "cc-brief: no such board" >&2; exit 1; fi
[ "${1:-}" = ready ] || exit 0
[ -n "${CC_FAKE_READY:-}" ] && printf '%s\n' "$CC_FAKE_READY"
exit 0
"""

FAKE_BROKER = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub for `cc-broker ledger --once <target> "text"`: records the call the way the real door would be
# called, so a held tick can be shown to be WRITTEN DOWN and not merely dropped. The real one owns events.md.
set -u
printf 'broker %s\n' "$*" >> "$CC_FAKE_CALLS"
echo ledger
"""

FAKE_PAUSE = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub for `cc-pause is <target>`: exit 0 when that target's PROJECT is in $CC_FAKE_PAUSED. It derives
# the repo the way the real one does — `myrepo`, `myrepo/track` and `myrepo@alias` are all the project `myrepo` —
# because the thing under test here is that the pulse hands over the whole target and lets cc-pause do that.
set -u
[ "${1:-}" = is ] || exit 2
t="${2:-}"; t="${t%%/*}"; t="${t%%@*}"
case " ${CC_FAKE_PAUSED:-} " in *" $t "*) exit 0;; esac
exit 1
"""

FAKE_CONTEXT = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# `cc-context --all`: $CC_FAKE_CTX is the TSV, $CC_FAKE_CTX_BROKEN makes it unable to answer.
[ -n "$CC_FAKE_CTX_BROKEN" ] && exit 1
printf '%s' "$CC_FAKE_CTX"
"""

FAKE_PS = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub for the two `ps` calls this file makes: passed straight to the real ps, except pane_age's own
# `-p $CC_FAKE_PS_GONE` etimes query, which is made to fail — standing in for a pid that raced or exited between
# the tmux snapshot and this call, the one pane_age failure mode nothing else here answers for.
set -u
if [ "${1:-}" = "-o" ] && [ "${2:-}" = "etimes=" ] && [ "${3:-}" = "-p" ] && [ -n "${CC_FAKE_PS_GONE:-}" ] \
   && [ "${4:-}" = "$CC_FAKE_PS_GONE" ]; then
  exit 1
fi
exec "${CC_REAL_PS:-/usr/bin/ps}" "$@"
"""

FAKE_HANDOFF = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub: `--status <target>` exits 0 when that target is in $CC_FAKE_HANDOFF (a handoff is open), 1 not,
# and 2 when $CC_FAKE_HANDOFF_BROKEN is set — the CLI is there but cannot answer.
set -u
[ -n "${CC_FAKE_HANDOFF_BROKEN:-}" ] && exit 2
case " ${CC_FAKE_HANDOFF:-} " in *" ${2:-} "*) exit 0;; esac
exit 1
"""


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
